  <data key="d0">entity_name</data>
  <data key="d1">entity_type</data>
  <data key="d2">Entity name is an abstract placeholder rather than a concrete, defined entity. In the source material it appears only as a generic label, and no substantive details about its nature, properties, history, functions, relationships, or classification are provided.

All entries in the description list consist almost exclusively of the repeated term “entity_description,” sometimes shown with minor typographic variations such as back‑ticks, quotation marks, a trailing period, or escaped characters. These placeholder texts convey no additional information about the entity.

Because the only information supplied is this repetitive placeholder text, Entity name cannot be placed into any predefined category such as Person, Creature, Organization, Location, Event, Concept, Method, Content, Data, Artifact, NaturalObject, or Other. The term serves solely as a stand‑in meant to be replaced by an actual, meaningful name in a finalized dataset, leaving the entity undefined and abstract.&lt;SEP&gt;entity_description&lt;SEP&gt;entity_description&lt;SEP&gt;entity_description&lt;SEP&gt;entity_description&lt;SEP&gt;entity_description. In previous answer, we had many lines. Need to verify entity_type values are from allowed list: Person,Creature,Organization,Location,Event,Concept,Method,Content,Data,Artifact,NaturalObject,Other. We used Method, Concept, NaturalObject, Artifact, Person. All allowed.&lt;SEP&gt;entity_description</data>
