<node id="Peripheral Blood Vessels">
  <data key="d0">Peripheral Blood Vessels</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a3e3297cd7a04555f40229593145f2e2</data>
  <data key="d2">Sympathetic nerves cause constriction or spasms in peripheral blood vessels that supply the extremities.</data>
  <data key="d1">UNKNOWN</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786373169</data>
</node>
<node id="Sympathetic Nerves">
  <data key="d0">Sympathetic Nerves</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a3e3297cd7a04555f40229593145f2e2</data>
  <data key="d2">Sympathetic nerves cause constriction or spasms in peripheral blood vessels that supply the extremities.</data>
  <data key="d1">UNKNOWN</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786373169</data>
</node>
<node id="Hands">
  <data key="d0">Hands</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a3e3297cd7a04555f40229593145f2e2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e261bddabf1598318602d5ab776f60c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-097e88fc7e1ff542a919812e0d0d508a</data>
  <data key="d2">Spasms in peripheral blood vessels decrease their internal diameter, reducing blood flow to the hands.&lt;SEP&gt;Hands are distal parts of the arms that can be amputated.&lt;SEP&gt;Hands are body parts where reflexology pressure points are located.</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786426920</data>
</node>
<node id="Acromegaly">
  <data key="d0">Acromegaly</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a3e3297cd7a04555f40229593145f2e2</data>
  <data key="d2">Acromegaly can affect the heart, contributing to early death if untreated.</data>
  <data key="d1">UNKNOWN</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786373170</data>
</node>
<node id="Heart">
  <data key="d0">Heart</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5f36c10fba48ed73bf1306763cd71ea5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a3b3c33dc539d83724153cf8bd92691&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f414d4fa06aded8c4be440d5be905e48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c02740b694bb0616d4b4005a09fd946b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3e3297cd7a04555f40229593145f2e2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5922b682f2c38e37dd9bd7f8f614180&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-910d4bcf6932bd29fd1d161e11852b46&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f81367bc23721681b080e8e55f88ab71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9afe775e3de6a2ede3813b294c2d7085&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3cdce04fe6b7fc2f2383ff5401c7803f</data>
  <data key="d2">Heart is the central muscular organ that pumps blood throughout the body, thereby generating the blood pressure required for circulatory function.  Its contractile muscle tissue derives energy from oxygen‑rich arterial blood that delivers nutrients, allowing it to maintain continuous rhythmic contractions that move blood through the systemic and pulmonary circuits.

When the heart’s pumping ability is compromised, the condition is defined as heart failure, a clinical syndrome characterized by inadequate perfusion of tissues and fluid accumulation.  The organ’s function can be deliberately halted for a short period during procedures such as heart valve repair surgery, after which normal rhythm is restored.

The heart is also a target of several systemic diseases.  In untreated acromegaly, excess growth‑hormone‑mediated cardiac remodeling can lead to early mortality.  Alagille syndrome, a genetic disorder affecting multiple organ systems, frequently involves congenital heart anomalies that may require medical or surgical management.  In various pathological states the heart may become strained, reflecting increased workload or pressure overload that can precipitate structural and functional deterioration.&lt;SEP&gt;The heart is the organ responsible for pumping blood, whose outer membrane (pericaridium) may be removed during extrapleural pneumonectomy.&lt;SEP&gt;The heart is the muscular organ that pumps blood into the arterial system.</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786422887</data>
</node>
<node id="Feet">
  <data key="d0">Feet</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a3e3297cd7a04555f40229593145f2e2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e261bddabf1598318602d5ab776f60c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-097e88fc7e1ff542a919812e0d0d508a</data>
  <data key="d2">Spasms in peripheral blood vessels decrease their internal diameter, reducing blood flow to the feet.&lt;SEP&gt;Feet are distal parts of the legs that can be amputated.&lt;SEP&gt;Feet are body parts where reflexology pressure points are located.</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786426920</data>
</node>
<node id="Lungs">
  <data key="d0">Lungs</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-448825a5baf21347c5a8fd84c362669b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ebc91deb19a2b42710150361b08958e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-976f1e53f9085499934de45456b8e026&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3e3297cd7a04555f40229593145f2e2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-086f817567ac7efd026aad0f965d640e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6007bd886198fad2e92df82e7baf0b56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7dcd996730cbdde5a41d3f86b16d8074&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9afe775e3de6a2ede3813b294c2d7085&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-436bf04244acb1c38f4b291e7d30ec94&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5bc0e87b467a3072004e86dd7a21ec8c</data>
  <data key="d2">Lungs are a pair of vital respiratory organs that facilitate gas exchange between the external environment and the bloodstream. They are enclosed by the pleura, a thin serous membrane that separates each lung from the thoracic cavity and provides a lubricated surface for smooth movement during breathing.  

Because they are the primary site of ventilation, the lungs are the main target for a variety of infectious and inflammatory diseases. Aspergillosis, a fungal infection, commonly involves the lungs and can cause serious respiratory impairment. Bacterial pneumococcal pneumonia also commonly infects the lungs, leading to the accumulation of inflammatory fluid within the alveolar spaces. In hypersensitivity pneumonitis, an immune‑mediated reaction to inhaled antigens, inflammation occurs chiefly in the lung tissue.  

The lungs are the source of hemoptysis, the coughing up of blood or blood‑stained sputum, when damage to pulmonary blood vessels occurs. They can also become strained by excessive mechanical stress, such as that produced by intense physical exertion or chronic respiratory loads.  

Endocrine disorders can further impact lung health. In individuals with untreated acromegaly—a condition of excess growth hormone—pulmonary changes may develop and contribute to premature mortality if not managed.  

Overall, the lungs’ paired structure, pleural lining, and central role in respiration make them susceptible to a wide spectrum of pathological processes, ranging from infectious agents and immune reactions to hormonal disorders and mechanical strain.&lt;SEP&gt;Lungs are the paired respiratory organs responsible for gas exchange.&lt;SEP&gt;The lungs are the organs whose function is measured by pulmonary function tests.</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786425953</data>
</node>
<node id="Large Intestine">
  <data key="d0">Large Intestine</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a3e3297cd7a04555f40229593145f2e2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7294fc7c2531b2723bf6002b6e65eee2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ec2b081d8d4ebb2f1d50934b4b4d93dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-039b8130a20b6f937b48c9084aa01ee2</data>
  <data key="d2">Acromegaly can lead to development of cancer in the large intestine, contributing to early mortality if untreated.&lt;SEP&gt;The large intestine is the distal part of the digestive tract that can be connected to a fistula in high anal atresia.&lt;SEP&gt;The large intestine is a major part of the gastrointestinal tract mentioned as a key term.&lt;SEP&gt;The large intestine is the final part of the digestive tract that includes the colon, sigmoid colon, and rectum.</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786427056</data>
</node>
<node id="Gigantism">
  <data key="d0">Gigantism</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a3e3297cd7a04555f40229593145f2e2</data>
  <data key="d2">Acromegaly and gigantism are listed together as related disease/condition entries.</data>
  <data key="d1">UNKNOWN</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786373176</data>
</node>
<node id="Raynauds Disease">
  <data key="d0">Raynauds Disease</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a3e3297cd7a04555f40229593145f2e2</data>
  <data key="d2">Raynaud’s disease differs from acrocyanosis by causing white and red skin coloration phases instead of only bluish discoloration.</data>
  <data key="d1">UNKNOWN</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786373180</data>
</node>
<node id="Calcium Channel Blockers">
  <data key="d0">Calcium Channel Blockers</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a3e3297cd7a04555f40229593145f2e2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e534c2f61dae08dd6e4ae98c00c82c7d</data>
  <data key="d2">Calcium channel blockers reduce the symptoms of acrocyanosis in most cases.&lt;SEP&gt;Medications that improve flexibility of heart muscle walls used to treat hypertrophic cardiomyopathy.</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786410466</data>
</node>
<node id="Alpha-One Antagonists">
  <data key="d0">Alpha-One Antagonists</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a3e3297cd7a04555f40229593145f2e2</data>
  <data key="d2">Alpha-one antagonists reduce the symptoms of acrocyanosis in most cases.</data>
  <data key="d1">UNKNOWN</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786373184</data>
</node>
<node id="Achondroplasia">
  <data key="d0">Achondroplasia</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Achondroplasia is a genetic disease that is the most common cause of dwarfism, characterized by abnormal short stature and disturbed cartilage development.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0df4e770c55ce326a53bf6ab0d1f1b74</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786373203</data>
</node>
<node id="Dwarfism">
  <data key="d0">Dwarfism</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Dwarfism refers to significantly abnormal short stature, most commonly caused by Achondroplasia.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0df4e770c55ce326a53bf6ab0d1f1b74</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786373203</data>
</node>
<node id="Cartilage">
  <data key="d0">Cartilage</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Cartilage is a fibrous connective tissue that normally produces bone; its development is disturbed in Achondroplasia.&lt;SEP&gt;Cartilage is the front portion of the nasal septum that relies on blood vessels in the mucus membranes for nutrition.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6c5f40938298f68915d43c1e7cdd62e8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0df4e770c55ce326a53bf6ab0d1f1b74</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786419621</data>
</node>
<node id="Bone">
  <data key="d0">Bone</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Bone is the hardened form of cartilage after calcium deposition; bone growth is impaired in Achondroplasia.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone forms the posterior portion of the nasal septum.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6c5f40938298f68915d43c1e7cdd62e8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0df4e770c55ce326a53bf6ab0d1f1b74</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786419621</data>
</node>
<node id="Calcium">
  <data key="d0">Calcium</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Calcium is a mineral deposited in cartilage to harden it into bone.&lt;SEP&gt;Calcium is an essential mineral that plays roles in bone development, tooth formation, and various body functions, and its blood level defines hypercalcemia.&lt;SEP&gt;Calcium is an essential mineral involved in bone development, tooth formation, and various body functions, with elevated blood levels defining hypercalcemia.&lt;SEP&gt;Calcium deposits are found in the elastic fibers of affected tissues in Pseudoxanthoma Elasticum.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ccd7967460bd192566c346ccf6adf5af&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ebfd4a7217e0e17b00a9c2caf49c2826&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0df4e770c55ce326a53bf6ab0d1f1b74</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786425466</data>
</node>
<node id="Genetic Defect">
  <data key="d0">Genetic Defect</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A genetic defect is the underlying cause of Achondroplasia and follows a dominant inheritance pattern.&lt;SEP&gt;The genetic defect in ALD reduces the ability to degrade very long chain fatty acids, leading to their buildup.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic defect refers to inherited abnormalities that can cause hypothyroidism in newborns.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic defect refers to inherited abnormalities that may combine with sporadic mutations in Parkinson’s disease.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic defect refers to an underlying hereditary abnormality that may increase tendency to cardiac arrhythmias in individuals who develop LQTS after medication exposure.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a88e9cf64ee28573945491871b0031d7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b1571076590b816bc3bb0de3b67b9a10&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2887ebc17d5fa167b3627fd14696de82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b1385133e299def4ed989633bdbd7168&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0df4e770c55ce326a53bf6ab0d1f1b74</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786424539</data>
</node>
<node id="Dominant Trait">
  <data key="d0">Dominant Trait</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Dominant trait describes the inheritance pattern whereby any individual with the genetic defect displays the disorder.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0df4e770c55ce326a53bf6ab0d1f1b74</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786373203</data>
</node>
<node id="Mutation">
  <data key="d0">Mutation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Mutation refers to a new change in a gene that can cause Achondroplasia, with frequency increasing with paternal age.&lt;SEP&gt;Mutation is a change in DNA that can cause Alagille Syndrome.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c02740b694bb0616d4b4005a09fd946b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0df4e770c55ce326a53bf6ab0d1f1b74</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786376587</data>
</node>
<node id="Paternal Age Effect">
  <data key="d0">Paternal Age Effect</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The increase in frequency of the Achondroplasia defect with increasing age of the father.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0df4e770c55ce326a53bf6ab0d1f1b74</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786373204</data>
</node>
<node id="X-ray Examination">
  <data key="d0">X-ray Examination</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">X-ray examination is a diagnostic method that reveals characteristic short and thick bones in Achondroplasia.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0df4e770c55ce326a53bf6ab0d1f1b74</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786373204</data>
</node>
<node id="Antibiotics">
  <data key="d0">Antibiotics</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Antibiotics are a class of antimicrobial drugs primarily prescribed to treat acute or chronic infections caused by bacteria, a subset of infectious diseases. By inhibiting bacterial growth or directly killing bacterial cells, they enable the body to eliminate pathogenic microorganisms and are also employed to prevent infections in certain clinical contexts.  

In clinical practice antibiotics are used across a wide range of patient groups and conditions. They treat acute otitis media in children with Achondroplasia, manage acute lymphangitis, and address infections that commonly affect elderly patients. They are administered to premature infants, to patients following portal‑vein bypass surgery, and as prophylaxis after surgical amputation or during procedures for individuals with heart murmurs. Antibiotics also play a role in preventing infection in the setting of graft‑versus‑host disease and in the treatment of infectious arthritis, lung infections, and associated pleural effusions.  

Beyond their core antibacterial activity, antibiotics have occasional secondary applications, such as managing the syndrome of inappropriate antidiuretic hormone secretion (SIADH) and treating certain protozoal infections. However, their use is not universally beneficial; for example, some antibiotics can exacerbate an Escherichia coli infection, underscoring the need for careful selection of agents based on the specific pathogen and clinical scenario.  

Overall, antibiotics constitute essential medical agents used both to treat and, in many cases, to prevent bacterial infections across diverse patient populations and clinical situations.&lt;SEP&gt;Antibiotics are medications used to treat bacterial forms of prostatitis.&lt;SEP&gt;Antibiotics are medications given during rabies treatment to prevent secondary infections.&lt;SEP&gt;Antibiotics are drugs administered, often intravenously, to treat acute pyelonephritis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-448825a5baf21347c5a8fd84c362669b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3167aac9bbeb660fe5ef8b641759df31&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-04a89a85dc3be8d37412b0c61ebf99cb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0c63f65bb800d3d7fae8a0864fc3cdf3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2600a22cecd1107559c673a4542b5d0f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e7a69ca28e7676455bc1dd105b09f56c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a62b13a067bbff6ba6a45d67ea3a11ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-434d8d551fe8baa35b0eb7738c2dec25&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a31e273096780949be269771f5c7b3b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ebc93901c908c91f026b8f84613ebc71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bf8d23aad485a31e434afa128d656202&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9348993468d1735d7ee70858031d433&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b1b905af9bb1602798ef23bcfc2818c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-097e88fc7e1ff542a919812e0d0d508a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bead2f9ad4273b49f336c30d5e85e2f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2c10b11558c17065dc27bd62ccb0fb84&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0df4e770c55ce326a53bf6ab0d1f1b74&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-07cc217c989e2af1a805d2ee5017b0b3</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786426589</data>
</node>
<node id="Hydrocephalus">
  <data key="d0">Hydrocephalus</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Hydrocephalus is a complication of Achondroplasia involving excess cerebrospinal fluid.&lt;SEP&gt;Hydrocephalus is an abnormal expansion of the brain ventricles caused by accumulation of cerebrospinal fluid, classified as a disease/condition.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-414a580eb00a7d0d58696314c2aa7dfe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0df4e770c55ce326a53bf6ab0d1f1b74</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786409694</data>
</node>
<node id="Foramen Magnum">
  <data key="d0">Foramen Magnum</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Foramen magnum is an anatomical opening that can be affected in Achondroplasia.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0df4e770c55ce326a53bf6ab0d1f1b74</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786373204</data>
</node>
<node id="Spinal Canal">
  <data key="d0">Spinal Canal</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Spinal canal is the passage within the spine that may be narrowed in Achondroplasia.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0df4e770c55ce326a53bf6ab0d1f1b74</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786373204</data>
</node>
<node id="Vertebrae">
  <data key="d0">Vertebrae</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Vertebrae are the bones of the spine that can have abnormal curves in Achondroplasia.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0df4e770c55ce326a53bf6ab0d1f1b74</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786373204</data>
</node>
<node id="Bowed Legs">
  <data key="d0">Bowed Legs</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Bowed legs are a skeletal complication observed in individuals with Achondroplasia.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0df4e770c55ce326a53bf6ab0d1f1b74</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786373204</data>
</node>
<node id="Acute Otitis Media">
  <data key="d0">Acute Otitis Media</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Acute otitis media is a middle ear infection that children with Achondroplasia may develop.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0df4e770c55ce326a53bf6ab0d1f1b74</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786373204</data>
</node>
<node id="Short Limbs">
  <data key="d0">Short Limbs</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Short limbs are a characteristic symptom of Achondroplasia visible at birth.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0df4e770c55ce326a53bf6ab0d1f1b74</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786373204</data>
</node>
<node id="Large Head">
  <data key="d0">Large Head</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Large head is a characteristic symptom of Achondroplasia visible at birth.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0df4e770c55ce326a53bf6ab0d1f1b74</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786373204</data>
</node>
<node id="Chondrodystrophy">
  <data key="d0">Chondrodystrophy</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Chondrodystrophy is a group of disorders, including Achondroplasia, where cartilage development and bone formation are disturbed.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0df4e770c55ce326a53bf6ab0d1f1b74</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786373204</data>
</node>
<node id="Nerve Compression">
  <data key="d0">Nerve Compression</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Nerve compression is a complication of Achondroplasia that can cause neurological problems.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0df4e770c55ce326a53bf6ab0d1f1b74</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786373205</data>
</node>
<node id="Achalasia">
  <data key="d0">Achalasia</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Achalasia is a disorder mentioned in the prognosis section, treatable but can lead to esophageal carcinoma.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0df4e770c55ce326a53bf6ab0d1f1b74</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786373205</data>
</node>
<node id="Esophageal Carcinoma">
  <data key="d0">Esophageal Carcinoma</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Esophageal carcinoma is a potential cancer that may develop in patients with achalasia, affecting life expectancy.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0df4e770c55ce326a53bf6ab0d1f1b74</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786373205</data>
</node>
<node id="Acetaminophen">
  <data key="d0">Acetaminophen</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0eaf14f47f2b7374eca25847c8bbdd8d</data>
  <data key="d2">Acetaminophen does not reduce inflammation, unlike aspirin.</data>
