<node id="Renal System">
  <data key="d0">Renal System</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Renal System is the body system comprised principally of the kidneys together with their associated ducts—most notably the ureters and, in certain surgical or experimental contexts, connections to a segment of the ileum. Its core physiological responsibilities are to filter the blood, eliminate metabolic waste, regulate fluid and electrolyte balance, control blood volume and arterial pressure, and maintain acid‑base homeostasis; the filtrate produced by these processes ultimately becomes urine.

In clinical practice the Renal System is frequently referenced because it influences a wide range of laboratory and diagnostic evaluations. It appears in listings for general laboratory “tests,” hormone panels, amylase assays, and antidiuretic hormone (ADH) measurements, reflecting its role in endocrine regulation and digestive enzyme metabolism. Specific renal investigations include work‑up for acute post‑streptococcal glomerulonephritis, studies of renal amino‑aciduria, and the haptoglobin test. The system is also cited in the context of immune‑complex testing and assessment of immunosuppression‑related effects.

The Renal System can be affected by several metabolic and genetic disorders. Hereditary fructose intolerance may involve renal dysfunction, while hypercholesterolemia can manifest with renal symptoms such as polyuria and polydipsia. Renal involvement is noted in conditions featuring fluid accumulation such as ascites, and the system is sometimes perceived as impaired in individuals with hypochondriasis. Additional disease associations include pheochromocytoma, protein‑energy malnutrition, and its listing among body systems relevant to rectal cancer, portal vein bypass procedures, and considerations in cosmetic surgery.

Beyond purely renal concerns, the Renal System is referenced in broader clinical contexts. It is mentioned in relation to the Gleason Score, underscoring its indirect importance in prostate‑cancer grading, and it is a system examined during pelvic examinations and pelvic ultrasound assessments. In summary, the Renal System is the principal organ system responsible for kidney function and is integral to normal physiology as well as a diverse array of diagnostic, therapeutic, and surgical considerations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-506592426d0a0ade6d5d62f2ac15f607&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a05c379dcf013630337bc48911a2ab86&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-25a42943ad0f93e6631565dc2c851d36&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5972068248f50904396788f92782210f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b29429d857edf1192105634eda51b928&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96678691a6b9b46cb9e089fa58def217&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b6f6eb6351953e7ef79475828425af93&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1ccba52c964c301daf559927d7d7c5e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-36c53d0d3d0b154d1cafa4ae311eafc9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-857d781bfe6e2b60c49450d61d8306db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e6407dc79c35579b5387abf2b05a97d0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44b884468a26dc2957843352da600dce&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-627b1597e2ff9fd7f303859f27a292f7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-551add173e0a688b46340efd695e578b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7913b0884240a87bdd0d8c498ec19f82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-708af40669770fee42a9a9b052181fce&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6dcc52e74701dc594a5093895fbb8763&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2c10b11558c17065dc27bd62ccb0fb84&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-684465558da6f22c66a6b34b82cea78c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc48729584254d74fcf981c77bc52930&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a94c4bf64c517d45cc23342217a0e34f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-012e85679bddc890f1489e743c060e63&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-afbff98c4c1d63870e1df4480f9167fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3cd4e9159e066f72729a8b0e6c804cc7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-35c30232e67a5a9d2bf59484035c1a03&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-380c6702cc605a3cfe1200c7e02a7fb6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5922b682f2c38e37dd9bd7f8f614180&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8049c6dc0f71e37cbd8d3611d0687a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3b7e1ff6aefba50b9ab4cb48e8298cf0</data>
