</node>
<node id="Dermatological System">
  <data key="d0">Dermatological System</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Dermatological System refers to the integumentary organ system consisting of the skin—including its epidermal and dermal layers—subcutaneous tissue, and associated appendages such as hair, nails, and a variety of glands. The system provides protection against external injury, regulates temperature, and mediates sensory perception, and it is routinely catalogued in clinical taxonomies as a distinct body system that can be affected by pathological, procedural, and pharmacological factors.

Across a broad spectrum of medical contexts the Dermatological System is explicitly cited as a site of involvement. Infectious and inflammatory conditions that feature cutaneous manifestations include acute post‑streptococcal glomerulonephritis, cutaneous T‑cell lymphoma, hereditary fructose intolerance, graft‑versus‑host disease, and photosensitivity reactions. Metabolic disturbances such as hyperlipoproteinemia, protein‑energy malnutrition, and the dermatologic sequelae of Polycystic Ovary Syndrome (e.g., hirsutism and acne) also involve the skin. Autoimmune and cholestatic disorders such as primary biliary cirrhosis produce rash and dry skin, while psoriatic arthritis and Pseudoxanthoma Elasticum generate characteristic lesions, thickening, and inelastic folds.

Surgical, traumatic, and routine procedural interventions regularly engage the Dermatological System. Amputation, craniotomy, gastrostomy tube placement, and orthopedic surgeries such as hammertoe correction require skin incisions and postoperative wound care. Even non‑surgical examinations—pelvic exams, pelvic ultrasound, peripheral vascular assessments, and routine phlebotomy—necessitate consideration of skin integrity and appearance. The system is also referenced in procedural listings such as pleural biopsy entries and in cosmetic procedures that focus on skin health and aesthetics.

Pharmacologic agents frequently produce dermatologic adverse events, making the Dermatological System a key focus in drug safety profiling. Anticoagulants may cause bruising, skin necrosis, or other cutaneous reactions; anti‑hemorrhoid, anti‑nausea, and antimigraine medications have been linked to allergic rashes; and systemic antifungal therapies target skin infections directly. Consequently, monitoring skin health is essential whenever patients are diagnosed, treated, or observed for conditions that may extend beyond primary dermatologic pathology.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-506592426d0a0ade6d5d62f2ac15f607&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b83572ef3bc13b6de1b3591b9a778c3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5972068248f50904396788f92782210f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2718c6e6fa21ad7d51803e9bd95a0fa3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1a321ea72ebe4c42d67faa7f47af64c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8bcf4112e39ea15ac243ebe48a81015e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22d527eab17c7341eca433d361df1f72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd702733210b77bd85a84e3b0046cb69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0b6a561225b73fa4827b9e9a8a411e05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-36c53d0d3d0b154d1cafa4ae311eafc9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca82475bbfe64d98c513d42669176ff3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d89439564ae2dc33beae4237976b40b1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-857d781bfe6e2b60c49450d61d8306db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9cfffa1f33540095dd9f47cec4a53945&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-09ac7fad90c3ef5bbb1366ea8a473928&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-142d840737a92f2ba527c40fc2edcbe2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e6407dc79c35579b5387abf2b05a97d0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56ef10afa5a5d842dfa90eafc05da760&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-551add173e0a688b46340efd695e578b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ebfd4a7217e0e17b00a9c2caf49c2826&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e2668ae388ce75891b60c313b7cab62b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db342ca6f9a76a46729e0e30b5cde75c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-07cc217c989e2af1a805d2ee5017b0b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eff6cb4281c913c830bd641c706365c5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-edba93ae4e0e61889c3b13cfe47b1b13&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-afbff98c4c1d63870e1df4480f9167fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-086f817567ac7efd026aad0f965d640e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-097e88fc7e1ff542a919812e0d0d508a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-91b03362a1a14b83e88e498b918bc854</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
