<node id="Diagnosis">
  <data key="d0">Diagnosis</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Diagnosis is a systematic medical process used to identify diseases, disorders, or other health conditions by integrating a patient’s reported signs and symptoms with objective findings obtained from history taking, physical examination, and ancillary investigations. The process typically begins with a detailed interview that gathers information on exposures, travel, animal contacts, medication use, family history, and other relevant background. A thorough physical exam follows, aiming to detect observable abnormalities such as skin changes, neurologic deficits, cardiac murmurs, or ocular findings. Clinicians then employ targeted diagnostic tools—including laboratory analyses of blood, urine, or other bodily fluids; imaging studies such as magnetic resonance imaging (MRI), computed tomography (CT), and specialized scans; toxicology screens; and, when necessary, tissue biopsy—to confirm or narrow down possible etiologies. The ultimate goal of diagnosis is to reach an accurate, actionable conclusion that guides appropriate treatment and management.

The concept of diagnosis also encompasses the identification of specific conditions using tailored criteria. For porphyria, diagnosis relies on the recognition of characteristic symptoms, verification of a positive family history, and confirmation through laboratory tests that detect abnormal porphyrin metabolism. Post‑polio syndrome is primarily suspected on the basis of patient history when muscle weakness reappears decades after an initial poliomyelitis infection, often without the need for extensive testing. Presbyopia is identified during a routine eye examination that reveals a loss of near‑focus ability associated with age‑related changes in the lens. Priapism is diagnosed by clinical examination of the prolonged, often painful erection and by performing investigations—such as blood gas analysis or doppler studies—to determine the underlying type (ischemic or non‑ischemic). 

Diagnosis of psoriatic arthritis involves a combination of clinical observations of skin and nail psoriasis, laboratory evaluation showing a negative rheumatoid factor, and characteristic radiographic findings on X‑ray that demonstrate joint involvement distinct from rheumatoid arthritis. Psoriasis itself is diagnosed through medical history that uncovers triggering factors, together with a physical assessment that identifies typical erythematous, scaly plaques and distribution patterns. Ptosis diagnosis is made by observing a drooping eyelid and conducting specific tests (e.g., the levator function test or the Marcus Gunn test) to assess the degree of levator muscle weakness and to differentiate neurogenic from myogenic causes. 

Across all these examples, diagnosis consistently follows the core methodology of integrating subjective reports with objective data—whether laboratory, imaging, or functional testing—to produce a definitive identification of the health condition in question.&lt;SEP&gt;Process of identifying breast tumors through self-examination, professional exam, or mammogram.&lt;SEP&gt;Diagnosis involves gathering patient history, symptom details, and diagnostic testing to identify gastrointestinal disorders.&lt;SEP&gt;Diagnosis of streptobacillary rat-bite fever can be made by specific clinical and laboratory procedures.&lt;SEP&gt;Diagnosis involves screening, physical examination, laboratory tests, and visualization techniques to identify rectal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Diagnosis of rectal prolapse involves patient history, rectal examination in a squatting position, and confirmatory tests such as sigmoidoscopy and barium enema X‑rays.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0412c9964e570b59a9a74a4404773c0b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c10fb15c165fb1c6b20b0aa8847da7ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9787f8a8b14586557271753b09718b06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-13d7186c36bc114fd063d81d9835a694&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d028b3e3f4cefe67cb0e2fd00c4e75c0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5479288509bdc5dc1bd82e1d305f9ed0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2835448bb481a076c03436c11f787066&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aeeced344cdde493cb84ba3e9ab7759f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d5f8eb7b45a1ef3316ab36b4d963198&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd702733210b77bd85a84e3b0046cb69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1ccba52c964c301daf559927d7d7c5e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-405f3438c94303b670da4ac7ad2e23a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d82066907ae788a5c895e3b1bededab3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2df310a5eb710fa15878efbe7d25bc7d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c3d1367f180f5cdbc9f3386cadd299a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-145bdf1ac68f0b5798dcddc645b482c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3167aac9bbeb660fe5ef8b641759df31&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5eb339d7a28b5edfc594d9ad14afee5c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-adcf683ab3aafdf4309bc219710636d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2887ebc17d5fa167b3627fd14696de82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97bc31370eb8948b543aaf5f06eb9116&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b1385133e299def4ed989633bdbd7168&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0caa0df876843ee924756d881922e0a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-556598665bad24192bd85d9014d7ac2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bf2a4a7521a933797a5c97cd08b21a3d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c71b04609b9999bd4f4017776dc567f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2056dc204f71f79fef28e7940fdf482f</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786426920</data>
</node>
<node id="Prognosis">
  <data key="d0">Prognosis</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Prognosis is the medical term for the anticipated course and likely outcome of a health condition, encompassing the probability of recovery, survival, symptom control, remission, or progression. Clinicians formulate prognostic assessments by integrating disease‑specific factors such as stage and pathology, patient‑specific characteristics (age, comor‑bidities, genetics), the timing of diagnosis, and the availability and efficacy of therapeutic options. These predictions guide clinical decision‑making, inform patient counseling, and help allocate health‑care resources.

The expected prognosis varies widely among disorders. Rare, severe metabolic diseases such as childhood or neonatal adrenoleukodystrophy carry a poor outlook with limited survival, whereas individuals with albinism in the United States typically enjoy a normal lifespan. Neurological conditions illustrate divergent expectations: apraxia after stroke or developmental speech disorders often improves substantially, while apraxia linked to progressive neurodegenerative diseases like Alzheimer’s disease has a less optimistic trajectory. Chronic inhalational illnesses such as asbestosis are not curable, but prognosis focuses on symptom management and variable disease progression. Therapeutic advances can dramatically shift prognosis; highly effective antiviral regimens have turned hepatitis C from a potentially fatal infection into a largely curable disease. Specific clinical contexts further refine expectations: human bite wounds have a prognosis that depends on bite location and whether the biter is a child or adult, with adult closed‑fist injuries showing higher infection rates; pregnancies complicated by hyperemesis gravidarum can still result in the delivery of a healthy infant; hypothyroidism has a very good prognosis when hormone replacement is administered; infectious arthritis usually resolves favorably with prompt antibiotics and joint drainage; internet addiction disorder is generally responsive to treatment; pancreatitis outcomes are predicted by the extent of necrosis and hemorrhage; and poisoning outcomes range from complete recovery to death according to toxin, dose, and treatment timeliness.

Additional condition‑specific prognoses illustrate how the term is applied across specialties. Isolated polydactyly and syndactyly have an excellent prognosis, while post‑polio syndrome typically follows a good, slowly progressing course. Most patients with psoriatic arthritis experience a good prognosis with milder symptoms than those seen in rheumatoid arthritis. After ptosis correction, the prognosis is generally good, although perfect symmetry may not be achieved. Pyloroplasty surgery often provides a complete cure with few long‑term complications. Rat‑bite fever has an excellent prognosis when appropriate antibiotics are given, but outcomes are variable without treatment. Finally, rectal cancer prognosis is expressed as a statistical likelihood of treatment success that depends on the cancer’s stage at presentation.&lt;SEP&gt;Prognosis of rectal prolapse depends on age, general health, and extent of prolapse, with infants and children usually recovering completely without complications.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0412c9964e570b59a9a74a4404773c0b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b12e63d79b206980d9b85d5beb720662&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-79f9159512d902b809ff6ebeca7cf146&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-13d7186c36bc114fd063d81d9835a694&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d028b3e3f4cefe67cb0e2fd00c4e75c0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aeeced344cdde493cb84ba3e9ab7759f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d5f8eb7b45a1ef3316ab36b4d963198&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd702733210b77bd85a84e3b0046cb69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1ccba52c964c301daf559927d7d7c5e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d82066907ae788a5c895e3b1bededab3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2df310a5eb710fa15878efbe7d25bc7d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b1571076590b816bc3bb0de3b67b9a10&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4542d461496cd18b5417df49b1f50690&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c3d1367f180f5cdbc9f3386cadd299a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5eb339d7a28b5edfc594d9ad14afee5c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e7a69ca28e7676455bc1dd105b09f56c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b1385133e299def4ed989633bdbd7168&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0caa0df876843ee924756d881922e0a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bf2a4a7521a933797a5c97cd08b21a3d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c71b04609b9999bd4f4017776dc567f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56d4bbf8d05ae296dc53a12cb3d6bc91&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cea23812b7285588a42ea7c6a323d1b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2056dc204f71f79fef28e7940fdf482f</data>
