</node>
<node id="Biopsy">
  <data key="d0">Biopsy</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Biopsy is a diagnostic medical procedure that involves the removal of a small piece of tissue from a patient’s body for microscopic or laboratory analysis. The primary purpose of a biopsy is to obtain a representative sample of suspicious or affected tissue so that pathologists can examine cellular architecture, detect the presence of disease‑causing agents, and provide a definitive diagnosis.

The technique is employed across a wide range of clinical contexts. In oncology, biopsies are routinely performed to confirm malignancies such as head and neck cancer, prostate cancer, and other solid tumors, often when imaging studies are inconclusive or when a precise histological classification is required. In urology, a prostate biopsy may be guided by ultrasound to clarify a diagnosis when imaging findings do not definitively indicate cancer. Biopsies also serve a differential‑diagnostic role, for example, distinguishing cancerous cells from infections such as syphilis or from benign lesions like penile warts.

Beyond cancer detection, biopsy sampling is used to investigate inflammatory and autoimmune conditions. Tissue obtained from patients with acute lymphangitis can be examined for infectious agents, while biopsies of liver or biliary tissue help assess diseases such as Primary Biliary Cirrhosis. Renal biopsies are listed among key investigative tools for kidney disorders, including Polycystic Kidney Disease, enabling evaluation of cystic changes and other pathological features. Additionally, biopsy can be applied to detect immune complex deposition in various systemic diseases.

Overall, biopsy remains a fundamental, versatile diagnostic method that provides essential histopathological information, guiding treatment decisions and improving patient management across numerous medical specialties.&lt;SEP&gt;Biopsy is a medical procedure to remove tissue for diagnostic analysis, listed among key terms.&lt;SEP&gt;Biopsy is a tissue sampling procedure used to confirm the diagnosis of rectal cancer.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eff6cb4281c913c830bd641c706365c5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c54bf8feacba012d420dc1bafe3e309&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-870bb1b70305260567e1654eccb86793&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-04a89a85dc3be8d37412b0c61ebf99cb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-627b1597e2ff9fd7f303859f27a292f7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2c9e6467acd0c81d0621428aab938849&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1ccba52c964c301daf559927d7d7c5e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d855036a34135250bf6b6942f08845d7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8049c6dc0f71e37cbd8d3611d0687a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bead2f9ad4273b49f336c30d5e85e2f1</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786426768</data>
</node>
