<node id="AIDS">
  <data key="d0">AIDS</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">AIDS is a severe infectious disease characterized by immune deficiency and opportunistic infections.&lt;SEP&gt;AIDS is a disease that immunologic therapy aims to combat by strengthening the immune system’s natural defenses.&lt;SEP&gt;AIDS is a condition that can lead to nondeliberate immunosuppression.&lt;SEP&gt;Acquired immunodeficiency syndrome is a condition of severe immune suppression associated with a high prevalence of Pneumocystis pneumonia.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f57760db433207b2438ded5229d83be6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b29429d857edf1192105634eda51b928&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4746af750ee6290790a52a00be9ec1c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-86d3c253b310c081f68af15bdade4f77</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786421792</data>
</node>
<node id="Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV)">
  <data key="d0">Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV)</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) is the virus that causes AIDS by damaging the immune system.&lt;SEP&gt;Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) is a virus that causes AIDS.&lt;SEP&gt;Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) is a virus that utilizes protease in the final stages of its replication process.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-86d3c253b310c081f68af15bdade4f77&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b1b905af9bb1602798ef23bcfc2818c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a175dfbbb5bdb8e9c075eb55543a5a41</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786424775</data>
</node>
<node id="Immune System">
  <data key="d0">Immune System</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The immune system is the body’s complex network of defenses that protects against a wide variety of threats, including bacteria, viruses, fungi, parasites, cancer cells, and other foreign or aberrant substances. It continuously monitors internal homeostasis, identifies invading pathogens or abnormal cells, and mounts responses that involve innate mechanisms (such as phagocytosis and inflammation) and adaptive processes, notably the production of antibodies after exposure to antigens. These responses underlie phenomena such as hypersensitivity pneumonitis, where the system generates specific antibodies to inhaled antigens, as well as broader immune activities that can result in allergy, transplant rejection, and autoimmune disease.

When the immune system functions properly, it can eliminate infected or malignant cells and maintain tissue integrity. However, its effectiveness can be limited against certain pathogens, such as anaerobic bacteria, and can be compromised by a range of conditions. Infection with HIV directly damages immune cells, leading to heightened susceptibility to opportunistic infections and cancers. Genetic disorders such as Ataxia‑telangiectasia produce deficient immune responses, while progressive loss of immune competence is observed in Hodgkin’s lymphoma. Immunosuppression—whether iatrogenic, through drugs that inhibit immune activity, or disease‑induced—reduces the system’s ability to fight disease and increases the risk of infections like aspergillosis. In autoimmune hepatitis, the system mistakenly attacks liver tissue, illustrating how dysregulated immunity can target self.

The immune system also participates in pathological processes that are not purely protective. In polycystic kidney disease, immune cells attempt to remove renal cysts, but this effort can inadvertently damage healthy kidney tissue. Conversely, the system can be deliberately modulated for therapeutic benefit. Immunologic therapies aim to enhance disease‑fighting capacity, while specific agents such as the medicinal fungus Cordyceps, when combined with H‑2 blockers, have been reported to influence immune activity, suggesting potential avenues for adjusting immune responses in clinical settings. Immunosuppressant drugs, on the other hand, intentionally inhibit immune function to prevent graft rejection or control autoimmune flare‑ups, albeit at the cost of increased infection risk.

Overall, the immune system serves as the body’s primary defense mechanism, capable of recognizing and eliminating a broad spectrum of threats, but its balance is delicate. Both deficiency and overactivity can lead to disease, making precise regulation—whether through natural homeostatic processes or medical intervention—a central focus of contemporary biomedical research.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-976f1e53f9085499934de45456b8e026&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1acfa8b3bcf6abb174b1db6c3878690&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4746af750ee6290790a52a00be9ec1c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3978e5151245a8ce567b224992eabb69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b29429d857edf1192105634eda51b928&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb8410ab80dc1278656666cc6ce9a2b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6007bd886198fad2e92df82e7baf0b56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-91f1f0521effbc46ec1f542169ae21ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fea6119439079b171fb416dd0c1a0f6a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-86d3c253b310c081f68af15bdade4f77&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d855036a34135250bf6b6942f08845d7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8049c6dc0f71e37cbd8d3611d0687a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aeb4dffb5ad505f4839faaa747910b3c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d88b3e0c879cd2c524008f697f6859f7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-01a42939570e7d067a66881fea563910</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
