<node id="Liver">
  <data key="d0">Liver</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Liver is a vital organ that performs a wide range of metabolic, storage, and detoxification functions and is the target of several genetic, infectious, and toxic diseases. It metabolizes alcohol, breaking it down into less harmful substances, and it also stores glycogen, serving as a key reservoir of glucose that can become enlarged in glycogen‑storage diseases (GSD). The liver receives the haptoglobin‑hemoglobin complex from macrophages for processing, playing an essential role in recycling iron and clearing hemoglobin breakdown products from the bloodstream.

Because of its central role in metabolism and immune surveillance, the liver is frequently affected by disease. In Alagille syndrome, a genetic disorder, the liver sustains damage due to abnormal bile duct development. Hepatitis C infection primarily inflames the liver, leading to chronic inflammation and potential long‑term injury. Alcoholic hepatitis results from the toxic effects of excessive alcohol consumption, directly harming liver tissue. Autoimmune hepatitis causes the organ to become inflamed as the immune system mistakenly attacks hepatic cells, often necessitating biopsy and therapeutic intervention. Hepatitis E virus replicates mainly in the liver, where it can also cause inflammation and damage.

Overall, the liver’s functions in alcohol metabolism, glycogen storage, hemoglobin‑heme processing, and immune regulation make it a focal point for a spectrum of conditions—from genetic syndromes like Alagille and GSD to viral infections such as hepatitis C and E, as well as alcohol‑related and autoimmune inflammatory diseases.&lt;SEP&gt;The liver is the organ inflamed in hepatitis D and can develop cirrhosis.&lt;SEP&gt;The liver is a large internal organ weighing about 3 lb, performing over 100 functions including bile formation, detoxification, vitamin storage, and metabolism of carbohydrates, fats, and proteins.&lt;SEP&gt;The liver is the organ affected by Hepatitis A, where the virus multiplies and causes inflammation.&lt;SEP&gt;The liver is an organ where obstructions may be identified by PTHC.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6c5e751c6db20a49f7ca0a341eadc654&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4b14c26a47ca283ef7a5696f26a6c99c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f6deb35c161bfdda7d3d14623d9a411&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-79f9159512d902b809ff6ebeca7cf146&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-25a42943ad0f93e6631565dc2c851d36&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5eb339d7a28b5edfc594d9ad14afee5c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c02740b694bb0616d4b4005a09fd946b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3978e5151245a8ce567b224992eabb69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97bc31370eb8948b543aaf5f06eb9116&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3fe1b59b257b5d344c7de5181dbff9d0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6dcc52e74701dc594a5093895fbb8763&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3f088a5c1c6bbd06f9b18f4580c46043</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786419483</data>
</node>
<node id="Bile Ducts">
  <data key="d0">Bile Ducts</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Bile Ducts are ducts that transport bile from the liver to the gallbladder and small intestine.&lt;SEP&gt;Bile ducts are conduits for bile that may be examined for obstructions by PTHC.&lt;SEP&gt;Bile ducts are channels that transport bile; they are mentioned in the description but are unrelated to perforated eardrum.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c02740b694bb0616d4b4005a09fd946b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c5e751c6db20a49f7ca0a341eadc654&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0b9af0d9e28259265f55fcf42bbb724f</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786419756</data>
</node>
<node id="Gallbladder">
  <data key="d0">Gallbladder</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Gallbladder is an organ that stores bile.&lt;SEP&gt;Gallbladder stores and releases bile, which can act as a chemical irritant in peritonitis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c02740b694bb0616d4b4005a09fd946b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a3ecc34ed1c5383ab2125e8cf323393</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786419845</data>
</node>
<node id="Small Intestine">
  <data key="d0">Small Intestine</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Small Intestine is part of the digestive tract where bile aids digestion.&lt;SEP&gt;The small intestine receives digested food from the stomach and is part of the passage narrowed in pyloric stenosis.&lt;SEP&gt;The small intestine is the portion of the digestive tract where most nutrient absorption occurs, beginning with the duodenum.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c02740b694bb0616d4b4005a09fd946b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7db5d994bc1563d4fe5ab322fe7f3808&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2056dc204f71f79fef28e7940fdf482f</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786426607</data>
</node>
<node id="Skeleton">
  <data key="d0">Skeleton</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Skeleton is part of the body that can be affected by Alagille Syndrome.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c02740b694bb0616d4b4005a09fd946b</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786376585</data>
</node>
<node id="Bile">
  <data key="d0">Bile</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Bile is a fluid that aids digestion of fats.&lt;SEP&gt;Bile is a digestive fluid that can invade the peritoneum and contribute to ascites.&lt;SEP&gt;Bile is a yellow‑green fluid secreted by the liver that aids digestion of fats.&lt;SEP&gt;Bile is the digestive fluid whose flow can be slowed or stopped by obstructions identified by PTHC.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c02740b694bb0616d4b4005a09fd946b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c5e751c6db20a49f7ca0a341eadc654&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a05c379dcf013630337bc48911a2ab86&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97bc31370eb8948b543aaf5f06eb9116</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786419483</data>
</node>
<node id="Liver Damage">
  <data key="d0">Liver Damage</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Liver Damage is a condition caused by narrowed bile ducts in Alagille Syndrome.&lt;SEP&gt;Liver damage is a medical condition that the alanine aminotransferase test can detect, though ALT levels do not necessarily reflect its severity.&lt;SEP&gt;Organ injury caused by Hepatitis B infection as described in the text.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2679367b0beba9113c18be86c1ebf3c5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c02740b694bb0616d4b4005a09fd946b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a048e601dd7e1d6483996e8fa02d2546</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786420096</data>
</node>
<node id="Cirrhosis">
  <data key="d0">Cirrhosis</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Cirrhosis is scarring of the liver that can develop in Alagille Syndrome patients.&lt;SEP&gt;Cirrhosis is scarring of the liver that leads to portal hypertension and contributes to ascites.&lt;SEP&gt;Cirrhosis is severe liver scarring that can result from chronic Hepatitis C infection.&lt;SEP&gt;Cirrhosis is a key term related to liver disease.&lt;SEP&gt;Cirrhosis is a severe liver condition that can develop as a complication of chronic hepatitis B.&lt;SEP&gt;Cirrhosis is a related condition referenced in the key terms of Primary Biliary Cirrhosis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eff6cb4281c913c830bd641c706365c5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a05c379dcf013630337bc48911a2ab86&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5eb339d7a28b5edfc594d9ad14afee5c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c02740b694bb0616d4b4005a09fd946b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8edba73be60673b1668775ee43b5d8dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3fe1b59b257b5d344c7de5181dbff9d0</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786424271</data>
</node>
<node id="Jaundice">
  <data key="d0">Jaundice</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Jaundice is yellowing of skin and eyes, a symptom of Alagille Syndrome.&lt;SEP&gt;Jaundice is a symptom of hepatitis characterized by yellowing of the skin.&lt;SEP&gt;Jaundice is a symptom characterized by yellowing, noted as occurring around 12 weeks after infection.&lt;SEP&gt;Jaundice is a symptom of autoimmune hepatitis marked by yellowing of the skin and eyes.&lt;SEP&gt;Jaundice is a symptom of hepatitis E characterized by yellowing of the skin and eyes.&lt;SEP&gt;Jaundice is a condition causing yellowing of tissues, listed as a key term.&lt;SEP&gt;Jaundice is a condition characterized by yellow discoloration, associated with prematurity.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6c5e751c6db20a49f7ca0a341eadc654&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4b14c26a47ca283ef7a5696f26a6c99c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0c63f65bb800d3d7fae8a0864fc3cdf3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c02740b694bb0616d4b4005a09fd946b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8edba73be60673b1668775ee43b5d8dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3fe1b59b257b5d344c7de5181dbff9d0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3978e5151245a8ce567b224992eabb69</data>
