<node id="Blood">
  <data key="d0">Blood</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Blood is the circulating biological fluid of the body that serves as the primary transport medium for oxygen, carbon dioxide, nutrients, waste products, and hemoglobin throughout the vascular system. Produced in the bone marrow, blood is a cellular fluid containing red blood cells, white blood cells, and platelets, and it delivers the oxygen and nutrients needed for the energy metabolism of organs such as the heart. By carrying immune cells, blood plays a central role in the body’s defense mechanisms, although under normal conditions immune complexes are typically not detectable in the circulation. When transfused into a recipient, donor blood can also become a source of graft‑versus‑host disease.

In clinical practice, blood is routinely sampled for a wide range of diagnostic tests. It is the specimen used for amylase measurement, assessment of antidiuretic hormone (ADH) levels, and the Antimyocardial Antibody Test that evaluates cardiac‑related autoimmunity. Early‑stage pregnancy evaluation often incorporates blood‑based antenatal testing, and blood analysis is essential in diagnosing bleeding disorders such as allergic purpura. Specialized investigative procedures may manipulate blood—for example, withdrawing a patient’s blood, labeling it with a radioactive tracer, and re‑injecting it to locate gastrointestinal bleeding sites.

Blood’s properties are also relevant in medical procedures that alter its gas composition; extracorporeal circulation devices modify blood’s oxygen and carbon dioxide levels during surgeries such as cardiopulmonary bypass. Pathologically, blood can be expelled from the respiratory tract during hemoptysis, and it acts as the principal transmission medium for blood‑borne pathogens, most notably the Hepatitis C Virus, facilitating infection between individuals.&lt;SEP&gt;Blood is the circulatory fluid drawn during phlebotomy for analysis or treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Blood is the bodily fluid containing platelets and used for the platelet count test.&lt;SEP&gt;Blood is the circulatory fluid containing platelets, plasma, and cells, involved in clotting processes.&lt;SEP&gt;Circulatory fluid that can be invaded by pneumococcal pneumonia during systemic spread.&lt;SEP&gt;Blood is the circulating fluid that carries cells, proteins, and plasma throughout the body.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9b2f2e3046d0f775744775822b8b1381&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30ae64b33e92c1d03eab2e2ac4026629&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b6f6eb6351953e7ef79475828425af93&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ebc91deb19a2b42710150361b08958e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-821cf82982b22028efb7496d7551e952&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ebc37c967098587c747ad0f9bafa5164&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d89439564ae2dc33beae4237976b40b1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-776b91620577fff4149dbac4a89b46eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c07ccd2ac226d52ffc3c4068cddb090e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f414d4fa06aded8c4be440d5be905e48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-07c3f104d4a00ee29290d4bff7ae1489&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-07cc217c989e2af1a805d2ee5017b0b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5bc0e87b467a3072004e86dd7a21ec8c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f36c10fba48ed73bf1306763cd71ea5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5eb339d7a28b5edfc594d9ad14afee5c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-83aebc72b8b7f7141e25e88c35f39530&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-012e85679bddc890f1489e743c060e63&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8049c6dc0f71e37cbd8d3611d0687a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e46cef0181d6e65ea01360302ddd1f7b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-80e03e36082a52818b6740d8905c8a42</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786424624</data>
</node>
<node id="Urine">
  <data key="d0">Urine</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Urine is analyzed in diagnostic tests for Allergic Purpura.&lt;SEP&gt;Urine is a bodily fluid sampled for amylase testing and serves as a long‑term monitor of amylase levels.&lt;SEP&gt;Fluid whose concentration is increased by ADH action.&lt;SEP&gt;Urine is a bodily waste fluid through which excess porphyrins are excreted in porphyria.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b6f6eb6351953e7ef79475828425af93&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-012e85679bddc890f1489e743c060e63&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e46cef0181d6e65ea01360302ddd1f7b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-556598665bad24192bd85d9014d7ac2a</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786422919</data>
</node>
<node id="Blood Test">
  <data key="d0">Blood Test</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Blood Test is a broadly used diagnostic laboratory procedure in which a sample of a patient’s blood is collected—often after a period of fasting—and analyzed for a wide range of clinical markers. The test can serve both screening and confirmatory purposes across numerous medical specialties, providing objective data that assist clinicians in evaluating disease presence, severity, and progression.

Specific applications of the blood test include assessment for allergic purpura, wherein characteristic laboratory abnormalities support the diagnosis. Abnormal results may also signal hepatitis, reflecting hepatic injury or inflammation. Lipid profiling is routinely performed by measuring low‑density lipoprotein (LDL), high‑density lipoprotein (HDL), and total cholesterol levels from a fasting specimen, aiding cardiovascular risk stratification. In genetics, blood is a common source of DNA for parentage testing, offering a reliable means of establishing biological relationships. Neurologists may order blood tests to exclude alternative causes of Parkinson’s disease symptoms, using biochemical markers to narrow differential diagnoses. Measurement of creatine kinase (CK) levels is employed to evaluate muscle damage in conditions such as polymyositis and dermatomyositis, while testing for rheumatoid factor, although typically negative, can help differentiate psoriatic arthritis from other rheumatologic disorders.

In postoperative care, blood tests are frequently used to monitor vital organ function and metabolic status in patients who have undergone portal vein bypass surgery, ensuring early detection of complications and guiding ongoing treatment decisions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b785ff55416d26c9c9d2574c29ca4711&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2887ebc17d5fa167b3627fd14696de82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3cd4e9159e066f72729a8b0e6c804cc7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd702733210b77bd85a84e3b0046cb69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3fe1b59b257b5d344c7de5181dbff9d0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2c10b11558c17065dc27bd62ccb0fb84&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-346dbc5b46cc5130f0b88acb5bdd6aca&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e46cef0181d6e65ea01360302ddd1f7b</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786425313</data>
</node>
<node id="Urine Test">
  <data key="d0">Urine Test</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Urine test is a diagnostic method used to assess Allergic Purpura.&lt;SEP&gt;A urine test is a laboratory analysis that detects aminoaciduria to confirm Hartnup disease.&lt;SEP&gt;Urine tests are used to monitor vital functions in patients after portal vein bypass.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2c10b11558c17065dc27bd62ccb0fb84&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3a5fe7cfd2eb995bee47dc8e7a28fbbf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e46cef0181d6e65ea01360302ddd1f7b</data>
