  <data key="d5">1786411606</data>
</node>
<node id="Wheat">
  <data key="d0">Wheat</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Wheat is a food allergen mentioned in the text.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fea6119439079b171fb416dd0c1a0f6a</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786377853</data>
</node>
<node id="Soybeans">
  <data key="d0">Soybeans</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Soybeans are a food allergen mentioned in the text.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fea6119439079b171fb416dd0c1a0f6a</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786377853</data>
</node>
<node id="Criminal Justice System">
  <data key="d0">Criminal Justice System</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Criminal Justice System is a system mentioned as often being involved with individuals diagnosed with alcoholism.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0b5b4ede4683a3be477e4527fbbb8cad</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786377853</data>
</node>
<node id="Family Involvement">
  <data key="d0">Family Involvement</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Family involvement refers to the participation of family members in the treatment and management of alcoholism.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0b5b4ede4683a3be477e4527fbbb8cad</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786377853</data>
</node>
<node id="Immunotherapy">
  <data key="d0">Immunotherapy</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Immunotherapy (allergy shots) is a treatment method that alters antibody balance to manage allergies.&lt;SEP&gt;Immunotherapy is a key term denoting the therapeutic approach of Provenge.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fea6119439079b171fb416dd0c1a0f6a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea3519af09df48250c19cedc50f317f4</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786425689</data>
</node>
<node id="Avoidance">
  <data key="d0">Avoidance</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Avoidance is a treatment method involving the avoidance of allergens.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fea6119439079b171fb416dd0c1a0f6a</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786377853</data>
</node>
<node id="Allergy Shots">
  <data key="d0">Allergy Shots</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Allergy Shots are a form of immunotherapy involving incremental allergen injections.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fea6119439079b171fb416dd0c1a0f6a</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786377854</data>
</node>
<node id="Amblyopia">
  <data key="d0">Amblyopia</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Amblyopia is a severe decrease in vision in a healthy eye caused by the eye and brain failing to work together, leading to suppression of the blurrier image.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-49690ebe88a03e6a7140692753a7c25a</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786377976</data>
</node>
<node id="Lazy Eye">
  <data key="d0">Lazy Eye</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Lazy Eye is a common nonmedical term used to describe amblyopia, referring to the eye with poorer vision that does not seem to be seeing properly.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-49690ebe88a03e6a7140692753a7c25a</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786377976</data>
</node>
<node id="Strabismus">
  <data key="d0">Strabismus</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Strabismus is a misalignment of the eyes where they look in different directions, and is the most common cause of functional amblyopia.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-49690ebe88a03e6a7140692753a7c25a</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786377976</data>
</node>
<node id="Anisometropia">
  <data key="d0">Anisometropia</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Anisometropia is a condition where there is a difference in refractive states between the two eyes, serving as another cause of functional amblyopia.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-49690ebe88a03e6a7140692753a7c25a</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786377976</data>
</node>
<node id="Cataract">
  <data key="d0">Cataract</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Cataract is an eye condition listed among key terms related to visual disorders.&lt;SEP&gt;Cataract is an ocular obstruction that can eliminate the red reflex and is screened for by Red Reflex Testing.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-49690ebe88a03e6a7140692753a7c25a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3db6c2b2025b59f4b58a91f35ae965a1</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786426985</data>
</node>
<node id="Occlusion Therapy">
  <data key="d0">Occlusion Therapy</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Occlusion therapy is a treatment method used to manage amblyopia by covering the stronger eye to stimulate the weaker eye.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-49690ebe88a03e6a7140692753a7c25a</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786377976</data>
</node>
<node id="Visual Acuity">
  <data key="d0">Visual Acuity</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Visual acuity refers to the clarity of images received from the eyes, a component of overall vision.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-49690ebe88a03e6a7140692753a7c25a</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786377976</data>
</node>
<node id="Vision">
  <data key="d0">Vision</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Vision is the combination of visual acuity and the brain's processing of images from both eyes.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-49690ebe88a03e6a7140692753a7c25a</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786377976</data>
</node>
<node id="Eye">
  <data key="d0">Eye</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">The eye captures visual images, and dysfunction in coordination with the brain can lead to amblyopia.&lt;SEP&gt;The eye is the organ containing the lens, cornea, and retina, where hyperopia originates.&lt;SEP&gt;The eye is the organ of vision that contains structures such as the iris, pupil, crystalline lens, and ciliary muscles.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9787f8a8b14586557271753b09718b06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49690ebe88a03e6a7140692753a7c25a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f54ec7015ee2d6dc6b93427b653bc6f</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786423492</data>
</node>
<node id="Alzheimers Disease">
  <data key="d0">Alzheimers Disease</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Alzheimer’s Disease is the most common form of dementia in people aged 65 and older, an irreversible progressive neurological disorder characterized by neuron loss, brain atrophy, memory loss, and cognitive decline.&lt;SEP&gt;Alzheimer’s disease is a neurodegenerative disorder mentioned as a potential consequence of high homocysteine.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fd6df79e5db44ce548291f0e8d6d6cbc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c3046439ce6fa1d6d5f63b57269a3f9b</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786409175</data>
</node>
<node id="Alois Alzheimer">
  <data key="d0">Alois Alzheimer</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Alois Alzheimer was a German psychiatrist and neuroanatomist who identified abnormal brain plaques and tangles in a dementia patient, leading to the discovery of Alzheimer’s Disease.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fd6df79e5db44ce548291f0e8d6d6cbc</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786378005</data>
</node>
<node id="Frau D.">
  <data key="d0">Frau D.</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Frau D. was a 51-year-old woman whose brain autopsy revealed beta‑amyloid plaques and neurofibrillary tangles, providing the first diagnostic evidence of Alzheimer’s Disease.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fd6df79e5db44ce548291f0e8d6d6cbc</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786378005</data>
</node>
<node id="Beta‑Amyloid Plaques">
  <data key="d0">Beta‑Amyloid Plaques</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Beta‑amyloid plaques are abnormal clumps of material found in the brain tissue of Alzheimer’s Disease patients and serve as a diagnostic signature.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fd6df79e5db44ce548291f0e8d6d6cbc</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786378005</data>
</node>
<node id="Neurofibrillary Tangles">
  <data key="d0">Neurofibrillary Tangles</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Neurofibrillary tangles are tangled bundles of fibers observed in the brains of Alzheimer’s Disease patients and constitute a diagnostic hallmark.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fd6df79e5db44ce548291f0e8d6d6cbc</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786378005</data>
</node>
<node id="Neurons">
  <data key="d0">Neurons</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Neurons are nerve cells that degenerate and die in several brain areas during the progression of Alzheimer’s Disease.&lt;SEP&gt;Neurons are cells of the nervous system where the herpes virus can remain dormant during latency.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-784ba38e8bfe9ffbf875f5ee96725a4d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fd6df79e5db44ce548291f0e8d6d6cbc</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786400647</data>
</node>
<node id="Dementia">
  <data key="d0">Dementia</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Dementia is a broad category of cognitive impairment, of which Alzheimer’s Disease is the most common form.&lt;SEP&gt;Dementia is an organic neurological condition that must be ruled out when diagnosing paranoia.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-84ee6af5422b0bedd83a764af83874c3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fd6df79e5db44ce548291f0e8d6d6cbc</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786418666</data>
</node>
<node id="Genetic Factors">
  <data key="d0">Genetic Factors</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Genetic factors are proposed contributors to the development of Alzheimer’s Disease, even in sporadic cases.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic factors are hereditary elements that may contribute to the development of congenital hydrocephalus.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic factors are inherited elements that contribute to the development of insulin resistance.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic factors are implicated in the cause of psoriatic arthritis, suggesting hereditary susceptibility.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-bd702733210b77bd85a84e3b0046cb69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-414a580eb00a7d0d58696314c2aa7dfe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fd6df79e5db44ce548291f0e8d6d6cbc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e04fc4d9139221a4e00a150baf448e9</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786425303</data>
</node>
<node id="Environmental Factors">
  <data key="d0">Environmental Factors</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Environmental factors, including lifestyle and exposures, are suggested to play a role in the onset of Alzheimer’s Disease.&lt;SEP&gt;Environmental factors are non‑genetic influences that may contribute to the development of congenital hydrocephalus.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fd6df79e5db44ce548291f0e8d6d6cbc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-414a580eb00a7d0d58696314c2aa7dfe</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786409694</data>
</node>
<node id="Free Radicals">
  <data key="d0">Free Radicals</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Free radicals are highly reactive molecules that damage brain cells through oxidative processes, potentially contributing to Alzheimer’s Disease pathology.&lt;SEP&gt;Free Radicals are potentially damaging molecules that can cause damage to cell membranes, DNA, and proteins if not neutralized by antioxidants.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-dd48b3d64142d95730163769979b3bf0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fd6df79e5db44ce548291f0e8d6d6cbc</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786386826</data>
</node>
<node id="Serotonin">
  <data key="d0">Serotonin</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Serotonin is a neurotransmitter whose low levels are associated with Alzheimer’s Disease.&lt;SEP&gt;Serotonin is an excitatory neurotransmitter whose levels are increased by antidepressant drugs.&lt;SEP&gt;Serotonin is a neurotransmitter in the brain whose reabsorption is slowed by SSRI drugs, increasing its availability.&lt;SEP&gt;Serotonin is a neurotransmitter whose availability may be increased by appetite suppressant drugs.&lt;SEP&gt;Serotonin is a neurotransmitter with differing baseline levels in women and men.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fff53a187c372ebdee1b2526a7ad3f69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9102756028539b9228bf8362c3e96772&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fd6df79e5db44ce548291f0e8d6d6cbc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-99238eefb790bd6f517ddd61b96753ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-043b5eeeac66113551273095596cd117</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786420264</data>
</node>
<node id="Caregivers">
  <data key="d0">Caregivers</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Caregivers of Alzheimer’s Disease patients often experience anger, resentment, guilt, and hopelessness.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fd6df79e5db44ce548291f0e8d6d6cbc</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786378006</data>
</node>
<node id="Depression">
  <data key="d0">Depression</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Depression is a common symptom among Alzheimer’s Disease patients and their caregivers.&lt;SEP&gt;Depression is a psychiatric condition treated with antidepressant drugs, behavioral therapy, and exercise.&lt;SEP&gt;Depression is a mental health condition that SSRI medications are primarily prescribed to treat.&lt;SEP&gt;Depression is a mood disorder listed as a differential diagnosis to be ruled out when assessing hypochondriasis.&lt;SEP&gt;Depression is a mood disorder that phototherapy aims to relieve.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-684465558da6f22c66a6b34b82cea78c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fd6df79e5db44ce548291f0e8d6d6cbc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a63b2129b947c78015eda0d29076ec61&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-99238eefb790bd6f517ddd61b96753ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-043b5eeeac66113551273095596cd117</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786421117</data>
</node>
<node id="High‑Fat and High‑Calorie Diets">
  <data key="d0">High‑Fat and High‑Calorie Diets</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">High‑fat and high‑calorie diets have been suggested to increase the risk of developing Alzheimer’s Disease.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fd6df79e5db44ce548291f0e8d6d6cbc</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786378006</data>
</node>
<node id="Salt">
  <data key="d0">Salt</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Salt is mentioned as a possible dietary risk factor for Alzheimer’s Disease.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fd6df79e5db44ce548291f0e8d6d6cbc</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786378007</data>
</node>
<node id="Refined Carbohydrates">
  <data key="d0">Refined Carbohydrates</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Refined carbohydrates are listed as a possible dietary risk factor for Alzheimer’s Disease.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fd6df79e5db44ce548291f0e8d6d6cbc</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786378007</data>
</node>
<node id="Fish Consumption">
  <data key="d0">Fish Consumption</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Fish consumption is cited as a dietary factor that may reduce the risk of developing Alzheimer’s Disease.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fd6df79e5db44ce548291f0e8d6d6cbc</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786378007</data>
</node>
<node id="Traditional Treatment">
  <data key="d0">Traditional Treatment</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Traditional treatment refers to the conventional therapeutic approaches used for Alzheimer’s Disease, though no cure exists.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fd6df79e5db44ce548291f0e8d6d6cbc</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786378008</data>
</node>
<node id="Short-Term Memory Loss">
  <data key="d0">Short-Term Memory Loss</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Short-term memory loss is an early symptom of Alzheimer’s Disease.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fd6df79e5db44ce548291f0e8d6d6cbc</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786378008</data>
</node>
<node id="Systolic Blood Pressure">
  <data key="d0">Systolic Blood Pressure</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Systolic blood pressure is mentioned in the context of cardiovascular measurements but not directly linked to Alzheimer’s Disease.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fd6df79e5db44ce548291f0e8d6d6cbc</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786378008</data>
</node>
<node id="Kidney Biopsy">
  <data key="d0">Kidney Biopsy</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Kidney Biopsy is a medical procedure that examines kidney tissue for changes typical of Alport syndrome.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1dfd51ae04f9e4cb50f69eb42e877763</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786378171</data>
</node>
<node id="Pulmonary Edema">
  <data key="d0">Pulmonary Edema</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Pulmonary Edema is excess fluid in the lungs that may develop as a complication of Alport syndrome.&lt;SEP&gt;Pulmonary edema is the listed prognosis associated with the procedure.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1dfd51ae04f9e4cb50f69eb42e877763&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3b7e1ff6aefba50b9ab4cb48e8298cf0</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786425802</data>
</node>
<node id="Uremia">
  <data key="d0">Uremia</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Uremia is the accumulation of urea and waste products in the blood, associated with kidney failure in Alport syndrome.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1dfd51ae04f9e4cb50f69eb42e877763</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786378171</data>
</node>
<node id="Diuretic">
  <data key="d0">Diuretic</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Diuretic is a drug that increases urine production and is used to manage waste buildup in Alport syndrome patients.&lt;SEP&gt;A diuretic is a medication that may be given before and during craniotomy to manage fluid retention.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1dfd51ae04f9e4cb50f69eb42e877763&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-edba93ae4e0e61889c3b13cfe47b1b13</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786396260</data>
</node>
<node id="Dialysis">
  <data key="d0">Dialysis</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Dialysis is a medical process that filters blood when kidneys fail, used as a treatment for Alport syndrome.&lt;SEP&gt;Dialysis is a short‑term renal replacement therapy used in about half of Hemolytic-Uremic Syndrome cases.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1dfd51ae04f9e4cb50f69eb42e877763&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-434d8d551fe8baa35b0eb7738c2dec25</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786404893</data>
</node>
<node id="Albumin">
  <data key="d0">Albumin</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Albumin is a blood protein; low levels serve as a marker of nephrotic syndrome related to Alport syndrome.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1dfd51ae04f9e4cb50f69eb42e877763</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786378172</data>
</node>
<node id="Nephrotic Syndrome">
  <data key="d0">Nephrotic Syndrome</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Nephrotic Syndrome is a group of symptoms including proteinuria, low albumin, and swelling that may develop in Alport syndrome.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1dfd51ae04f9e4cb50f69eb42e877763</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786378172</data>
</node>
<node id="Nephritis">
  <data key="d0">Nephritis</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Nephritis is inflammation of the kidneys and is a condition managed in care for Alport syndrome.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1dfd51ae04f9e4cb50f69eb42e877763</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786378172</data>
</node>
<node id="High Blood Pressure">
  <data key="d0">High Blood Pressure</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">High Blood Pressure is a condition that may be controlled in patients with Alport syndrome.&lt;SEP&gt;High blood pressure is a condition where arterial pressure is elevated, potentially causing abnormal widening of the aorta.&lt;SEP&gt;High blood pressure is a symptom of preeclampsia involving dangerously elevated arterial pressure.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3cdce04fe6b7fc2f2383ff5401c7803f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1dfd51ae04f9e4cb50f69eb42e877763&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-83aebc72b8b7f7141e25e88c35f39530</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786422886</data>
</node>
<node id="Potassium Levels">
  <data key="d0">Potassium Levels</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Potassium Levels refer to the concentration of potassium in blood; elevated levels may be managed in Alport syndrome.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1dfd51ae04f9e4cb50f69eb42e877763</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786378172</data>
</node>
<node id="Alpha-fetoprotein (AFP) Test">
  <data key="d0">Alpha-fetoprotein (AFP) Test</data>
  <data key="d1">renalsystem</data>
  <data key="d2">relevant</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6edad0b009a6b67d4ddcad5b30d5bc8c</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786378181</data>
</node>
<node id="Amniotic Fluid">
  <data key="d0">Amniotic Fluid</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9ec71b0a5c34e36d6cca673cad0a2921&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6edad0b009a6b67d4ddcad5b30d5bc8c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-07c3f104d4a00ee29290d4bff7ae1489</data>
  <data key="d2">Alpha-fetoprotein (AFP) Test measures AFP levels in amniotic fluid to assess fetal conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;The fluid surrounding a fetus, listed as a key term associated with antenatal testing.&lt;SEP&gt;Amniotic fluid is the liquid surrounding the fetus, whose volume can be abnormal in polyhydramnios or oligohydramnios.</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786422809</data>
</node>
<node id="Maternal Blood">
  <data key="d0">Maternal Blood</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6edad0b009a6b67d4ddcad5b30d5bc8c</data>
  <data key="d2">Alpha-fetoprotein (AFP) Test measures AFP levels in maternal blood to assess fetal conditions.</data>
  <data key="d1">UNKNOWN</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786378183</data>
</node>
<node id="Ultrasound Test">
  <data key="d0">Ultrasound Test</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6edad0b009a6b67d4ddcad5b30d5bc8c</data>
  <data key="d2">When the AFP test indicates abnormally high AFP, an ultrasound test is used to diagnose the problem.</data>
  <data key="d1">UNKNOWN</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786378184</data>
</node>
<node id="Down Syndrome">
  <data key="d0">Down Syndrome</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6edad0b009a6b67d4ddcad5b30d5bc8c</data>
  <data key="d2">The AFP test is used as a screening tool for Down syndrome.</data>
  <data key="d1">UNKNOWN</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786378187</data>
</node>
<node id="Fetus">
  <data key="d0">Fetus</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1cc9b041882b1ffceb3664e339701aa0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6edad0b009a6b67d4ddcad5b30d5bc8c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f65898fb0918e956817b050a5bb5580</data>
  <data key="d2">Fetal distress or demise can raise AFP levels, impacting the test.&lt;SEP&gt;AFP levels measured by the test reflect conditions of the fetus.&lt;SEP&gt;The fetus is the developing baby inside the uterus that can be affected by maternal glucose levels.&lt;SEP&gt;The fetus is the developing baby inside the uterus, whose pressure can cause an incompetent cervix to open.</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786411902</data>
</node>
<node id="Amniotic Sac">
  <data key="d0">Amniotic Sac</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6edad0b009a6b67d4ddcad5b30d5bc8c</data>
  <data key="d2">Low fluid volume in the amniotic sac can cause elevated AFP levels, affecting the test results.</data>
  <data key="d1">UNKNOWN</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786378190</data>
</node>
<node id="Alopecia">
  <data key="d0">Alopecia</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Alopecia is a disease/condition defined as hair loss (baldness).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-79234b86eefaf132e002bda9d70d7428</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786378319</data>
</node>
<node id="Hair Loss">
  <data key="d0">Hair Loss</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Hair loss, also called baldness, is the condition described by alopecia.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-79234b86eefaf132e002bda9d70d7428</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786378319</data>
</node>
<node id="Male Pattern Baldness">
  <data key="d0">Male Pattern Baldness</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Male pattern baldness, also known as androgenic alopecia, is a normal form of hair loss in adult males.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-79234b86eefaf132e002bda9d70d7428</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786378319</data>
</node>
<node id="Androgenic Alopecia">
  <data key="d0">Androgenic Alopecia</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Androgenic alopecia is another term for male pattern baldness, characterized by hair loss on the top and front of the head.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-79234b86eefaf132e002bda9d70d7428</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786378320</data>
</node>
<node id="Metabolic Syndrome">
  <data key="d0">Metabolic Syndrome</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Metabolic syndrome is a cluster of conditions that increase risk for cardiovascular diseases and type 2 diabetes, reported as associated with androgenic alopecia.&lt;SEP&gt;Metabolic syndrome is a key term associated with appetite suppressant drugs, referring to a cluster of conditions that increase health risks.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-79234b86eefaf132e002bda9d70d7428&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fff53a187c372ebdee1b2526a7ad3f69</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786389547</data>
</node>
<node id="Cardiovascular Diseases">
  <data key="d0">Cardiovascular Diseases</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Cardiovascular diseases are a spectrum of heart and blood vessel disorders linked to metabolic syndrome.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-79234b86eefaf132e002bda9d70d7428</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786378320</data>
</node>
<node id="Diabetes Mellitus Type 2">
  <data key="d0">Diabetes Mellitus Type 2</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Diabetes mellitus type 2 is a chronic metabolic disease linked to metabolic syndrome.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-79234b86eefaf132e002bda9d70d7428</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786378320</data>
</node>
<node id="Alopecia Areata">
  <data key="d0">Alopecia Areata</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Alopecia areata is a form of alopecia mentioned in the text.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-79234b86eefaf132e002bda9d70d7428</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786378320</data>
</node>
<node id="Dermatologists">
  <data key="d0">Dermatologists</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Dermatologists are medical specialists skilled in diagnosing alopecia by visual examination.&lt;SEP&gt;Dermatologists are medical specialists who commonly employ cryotherapy to treat skin lesions.&lt;SEP&gt;Dermatologists are doctors specializing in skin diseases who can diagnose and treat genital herpes.&lt;SEP&gt;Dermatologists are medical specialists who can treat skin manifestations of Primary Biliary Cirrhosis using ultraviolet light.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eff6cb4281c913c830bd641c706365c5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-79234b86eefaf132e002bda9d70d7428&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-784ba38e8bfe9ffbf875f5ee96725a4d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fb3f69c0e24f7808954b601ab37de0ac</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786424269</data>
</node>
<node id="Allergy Tests">
  <data key="d0">Allergy Tests</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Allergy tests are diagnostic methods that assess a person's allergic sensitivity to commonly encountered environmental substances.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6f2eb90fc948c2cce9700f6264f9195a</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786378421</data>
</node>
<node id="Alpha1-Adrenergic Blockers">
  <data key="d0">Alpha1-Adrenergic Blockers</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Alpha1-adrenergic blockers are drugs that block alpha1 receptors in smooth muscle, leading to vasodilation and lowered blood pressure.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8428275c084088f8ee4bf166316b6e01</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786378519</data>
</node>
<node id="Alpha1 Receptors">
  <data key="d0">Alpha1 Receptors</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Alpha1 receptors are protein sites in smooth muscle cells that bind catecholamines to induce contraction.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8428275c084088f8ee4bf166316b6e01</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786378519</data>
</node>
<node id="Smooth Muscle">
  <data key="d0">Smooth Muscle</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Smooth muscle is a type of muscle tissue whose cells are affected by alpha1 receptors and catecholamines.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8428275c084088f8ee4bf166316b6e01</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786378519</data>
</node>
<node id="Catecholamines">
  <data key="d0">Catecholamines</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Catecholamines are stress hormones that normally act on smooth muscle via alpha1 receptors.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8428275c084088f8ee4bf166316b6e01</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786378519</data>
</node>
<node id="Hypertension">
  <data key="d0">Hypertension</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Hypertension, also known as high blood pressure, is a medical condition treated by alpha1-adrenergic blockers, diuretics, and low-salt diet.&lt;SEP&gt;Hypertension is a condition of high blood pressure.&lt;SEP&gt;Hypertension is high blood pressure identified as a cause of heart failure.&lt;SEP&gt;Hypertension is a severe disease characterized by consistently high blood pressure.&lt;SEP&gt;Hypertension is high blood pressure, a condition treated with diuretics among other therapies.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f8a0239df88721094e3c51f8a7373134&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b4f1384d965014ddcabd93b7495e501&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8428275c084088f8ee4bf166316b6e01&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-910d4bcf6932bd29fd1d161e11852b46&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-80337a816ac74c4f4d818c5c4c8c74d7</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786410760</data>
</node>
<node id="Benign Prostatic Hyperplasia">
  <data key="d0">Benign Prostatic Hyperplasia</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Benign Prostatic Hyperplasia (BPH) is a noncancerous enlargement of the prostate that primarily affects older men.&lt;SEP&gt;Benign Prostatic Hyperplasia is a noncancerous enlargement of the prostate gland that can restrict urine flow.&lt;SEP&gt;Benign prostatic hyperplasia is a noncancerous enlargement of the prostate that can be diagnosed via biopsy.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8428275c084088f8ee4bf166316b6e01&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-edfaa9dba99a768f9ffcf5c77b2d0525&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-abbfd6cbf4afd6662ba2707fbc17c147</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786424845</data>
</node>
<node id="Older Men">
  <data key="d0">Older Men</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Older men are the demographic group most commonly affected by Benign Prostatic Hyperplasia.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8428275c084088f8ee4bf166316b6e01</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786378520</data>
</node>
<node id="Prostate">
  <data key="d0">Prostate</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">The prostate is a donut-shaped gland located below the bladder that can enlarge in BPH.&lt;SEP&gt;The prostate is a gland in the male reproductive system that can be affected by antiandrogen drug treatments.&lt;SEP&gt;The prostate is a gland found only in men, surrounding the neck of the bladder and secreting fluid that mixes with sperm to become semen.&lt;SEP&gt;The prostate is a male reproductive gland located below the urinary bladder, behind the penis and in front of the rectum.&lt;SEP&gt;The prostate is a chestnut-shaped gland in the male reproductive system that can become enlarged, inflamed, or cancerous.&lt;SEP&gt;The prostate is a male reproductive gland located anterior to the rectum.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-870bb1b70305260567e1654eccb86793&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8428275c084088f8ee4bf166316b6e01&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-627b1597e2ff9fd7f303859f27a292f7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-427a659d0ef51d2588b292e6329fb824&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-abbfd6cbf4afd6662ba2707fbc17c147&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1ccba52c964c301daf559927d7d7c5e0</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786426766</data>
</node>
<node id="Bladder">
  <data key="d0">Bladder</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">The bladder is a hollow organ that stores urine and sits above the prostate.&lt;SEP&gt;The bladder is the urinary organ that can be directly connected to a fistula in high anal atresia.&lt;SEP&gt;The bladder is a muscular organ that stores urine before elimination.&lt;SEP&gt;The bladder can develop cystitis, a bacterial infection that may lead to pyelonephritis.&lt;SEP&gt;The bladder is an organ situated anterior to the rectum in males.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-edfaa9dba99a768f9ffcf5c77b2d0525&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-039b8130a20b6f937b48c9084aa01ee2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8428275c084088f8ee4bf166316b6e01&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2600a22cecd1107559c673a4542b5d0f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1ccba52c964c301daf559927d7d7c5e0</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786426766</data>
</node>
<node id="Low-Salt Diet">
  <data key="d0">Low-Salt Diet</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A low-salt diet is a lifestyle approach recommended as a first-line treatment for hypertension.&lt;SEP&gt;A low-salt diet is a dietary modification aimed at reducing fluid retention in ascites patients.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a05c379dcf013630337bc48911a2ab86&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8428275c084088f8ee4bf166316b6e01</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786390195</data>
</node>
<node id="Vasodilatation">
  <data key="d0">Vasodilatation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Vasodilatation is the physiological process of blood vessel widening that reduces vascular resistance and lowers blood pressure.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8428275c084088f8ee4bf166316b6e01</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786378520</data>
</node>
<node id="Amebiasis">
  <data key="d0">Amebiasis</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Amebiasis is an infectious parasitic disease in humans caused by the protozoan Entamoeba histolytica, spread via contaminated food, water, and person‑to‑person contact.&lt;SEP&gt;Amebiasis – Concept – disease.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f8c8cde44ecd33ed261dcf8056e92cec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02e1b300199e25407d954c8745371d3a</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786387294</data>
</node>
<node id="Entamoeba histolytica">
  <data key="d0">Entamoeba histolytica</data>
  <data key="d1">creature</data>
  <data key="d2">Entamoeba histolytica is a one‑celled protozoan parasite that invades and destroys intestinal tissues, causing amebiasis and intestinal ulceration.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-02e1b300199e25407d954c8745371d3a</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786378554</data>
</node>
<node id="Human beings">
  <data key="d0">Human beings</data>
  <data key="d1">creature</data>
  <data key="d2">Human beings are the only known host of the amebiasis organism and can acquire the disease through contaminated food, water, or contact with infected persons.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-02e1b300199e25407d954c8745371d3a</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786378554</data>
</node>
<node id="Gastrointestinal system">
  <data key="d0">Gastrointestinal system</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The gastrointestinal system is the body system affected by amebiasis, where the parasite invades intestinal tissues.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-02e1b300199e25407d954c8745371d3a</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786378554</data>
</node>
<node id="Intestinal wall">
  <data key="d0">Intestinal wall</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">The intestinal wall is the tissue of the intestines that can be invaded and destroyed by Entamoeba histolytica, leading to ulceration.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-02e1b300199e25407d954c8745371d3a</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786378554</data>
</node>
<node id="Tropical and Subtropical Areas">
  <data key="d0">Tropical and Subtropical Areas</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">Tropical and subtropical areas are regions where amebiasis occurs most frequently due to crowded living conditions and inadequate sanitation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-02e1b300199e25407d954c8745371d3a</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786378554</data>
</node>
<node id="Amebic Dysentery">
  <data key="d0">Amebic Dysentery</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Amebic dysentery is a severe form of amebiasis characterized by intense diarrhea and intestinal ulceration.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-02e1b300199e25407d954c8745371d3a</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786378554</data>
</node>
<node id="Asymptomatic Case">
  <data key="d0">Asymptomatic Case</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">An asymptomatic case refers to a person infected with amebiasis who shows no symptoms and may require no treatment.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-02e1b300199e25407d954c8745371d3a</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
