<node id="Mild Case">
  <data key="d0">Mild Case</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A mild case of amebiasis presents with few or no symptoms and often does not require medication.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-02e1b300199e25407d954c8745371d3a</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786378555</data>
</node>
<node id="Severe Case">
  <data key="d0">Severe Case</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A severe case of amebiasis involves intense symptoms, possible intestinal ulceration, and may be life‑ threatening.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-02e1b300199e25407d954c8745371d3a</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786378555</data>
</node>
<node id="Liver Abscess">
  <data key="d0">Liver Abscess</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A liver abscess is a collection of pus in the liver that can result from extraintestinal amebiasis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-02e1b300199e25407d954c8745371d3a</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786378555</data>
</node>
<node id="Extraintestinal Amebiasis">
  <data key="d0">Extraintestinal Amebiasis</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Extraintestinal amebiasis denotes infection by Entamoeba histolytica outside the intestine, requiring location‑specific drug regimens.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-02e1b300199e25407d954c8745371d3a</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786378555</data>
</node>
<node id="Medication">
  <data key="d0">Medication</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Medication refers to drugs used to kill the disease‑causing amebas in amebiasis treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Medication refers to drugs administered, often via catheter, to dissolve clots or thin the blood in arterial embolism treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;drugs that can prevent kidney stones.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d89b7b5832dd7ae55e8a5dd5f4a4a94a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02e1b300199e25407d954c8745371d3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a51c332fdfaf8c96be6ab58c809b221</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786409528</data>
</node>
<node id="Fluid Replacement Therapy">
  <data key="d0">Fluid Replacement Therapy</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Fluid replacement therapy is a medical method used to restore lost fluids in severe amebic dysentery.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-02e1b300199e25407d954c8745371d3a</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786378555</data>
</node>
<node id="Blood Replacement Therapy">
  <data key="d0">Blood Replacement Therapy</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Blood replacement therapy is a medical method used to restore lost blood in severe amebic dysentery.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-02e1b300199e25407d954c8745371d3a</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
