<node id="Psychotherapy">
  <data key="d0">Psychotherapy</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Psychotherapy is a psychological treatment method that employs mental and emotional techniques, rather than medication, to address a wide range of mental health conditions. It is classified as a therapeutic approach that operates through conversational and experiential strategies to promote insight, emotional regulation, and behavioral change.

The practice of psychotherapy is applied to specific problems such as anger management, where therapists help individuals recognize triggers, develop coping skills, and modify aggressive responses. It is also used with patients experiencing hysterical disorders, providing insight‑oriented interventions that aim to uncover underlying emotional conflicts. For individuals suffering from paruresis, the condition characterized by difficulty urinating in public, psychotherapy offers a non‑pharmacological option to reduce anxiety and improve functional coping.

In the realm of psychotic symptoms, psychotherapy serves as a method to assist patients dealing with paranoia and persecutory delusions, often integrating techniques that enhance reality testing and reduce distress. The approach is also incorporated into treatment plans for pervasive developmental disorders, offering structured support that can improve social communication and adaptive functioning.

Specific anxiety‑related conditions benefit from psychotherapeutic interventions as well. Phobias are commonly addressed with psychotherapy that may include cognitive‑behavioral techniques such as exposure therapy and cognitive restructuring to diminish fear responses. Postpartum depression is another area where psychotherapy is employed, either as a standalone treatment or in combination with medication, to alleviate depressive symptoms and support maternal wellbeing.

Overall, psychotherapy represents a versatile, evidence‑based treatment option for numerous mental disorders, employing psychological means to foster recovery, insight, and healthier patterns of thought and behavior.&lt;SEP&gt;Psychotherapy is the treatment of mental or emotional disorders and adjustment problems using psychological techniques instead of physical or biological means.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-80bdc5e62cc3de9c461969117469e796&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84ee6af5422b0bedd83a764af83874c3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f07ae0c28dc0d57b8d89d819ee884b1d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-011a30caa3696a5321ec2b965fba6473&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5e4b4100d5e6ad6ec77e0fab7182b1e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af3b4b939a2aeb8a2dffe85570c09b57&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8e02da87d46351e723d1298ee7642793&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-32995d0e87b6ef1faa098707755b406f</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786425944</data>
</node>
