<node id="Bacteria">
  <data key="d0">Bacteria</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c97017f932e4bda442f8550fa19f76e2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a05c379dcf013630337bc48911a2ab86&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97c12585544967d7c896d91a36a80447&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3da3e9ecf369a29de0664ed8a629a950&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-691c137b75421e29ad257ff721c4b853&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bf8d23aad485a31e434afa128d656202&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3fe1b59b257b5d344c7de5181dbff9d0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-47a3a83e48113d48f0a14a70afb7ebf8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5bc0e87b467a3072004e86dd7a21ec8c</data>
  <data key="d2">Bacteria are microscopic, single‑celled microorganisms that act as infective agents and are responsible for a wide range of bacterial infectious diseases. They reproduce rapidly, evolve quickly, and can acquire resistance genes, making them the primary contributors to the development of antibiotic resistance.

In clinical contexts, bacteria can cause visible symptoms such as redness in untreated wounds and act as irritants that provoke chronic inflammation. They are capable of invading the peritoneal cavity, potentially leading to ascites, and can inflame the liver, resulting in hepatic inflammation. Bacterial pathogens are also among the major classes that cause pneumonia, and their presence can be identified in sputum samples taken from patients with hemoptysis.  

Overall, bacteria’s rapid multiplication, genetic adaptability, and ability to colonize various body sites underpin their central role in both acute and chronic infectious processes across multiple organ systems.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacteria are normal skin inhabitants that can form plugs in sweat glands, contributing to the development of prickly heat.</data>
  <data key="d1">creature</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786424550</data>
</node>
