</node>
<node id="Antiemetics">
  <data key="d0">Antiemetics</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Antiemetics are a class of drugs given to treat nausea and vomiting, derived from the term emesis, available as over‑the‑counter or prescription‑only preparations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8189651eadb45a933adca499117c49ad</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786385578</data>
</node>
<node id="Nausea">
  <data key="d0">Nausea</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Nausea is a somatic symptom characterized by the sensation of needing to vomit, often described as an upset or queasy stomach that may progress to actual emesis. Because it reflects a strong urge to expel stomach contents, antiemetic medications are commonly administered to alleviate the discomfort and prevent vomiting.

Beyond its general definition, nausea appears as an adverse event in a variety of clinical contexts. In clinical trials evaluating anti‑inflammatory diets, participants have reported nausea as a side effect, indicating that dietary interventions can sometimes provoke this symptom. The condition is also recognized as a somatic manifestation of anxiety, where psychological stress produces an upset stomach and the accompanying urge to vomit.

Nausea is frequently documented among patients with liver‑related infections and disorders. Individuals infected with hepatitis B, hepatitis E, and those suffering from autoimmune hepatitis commonly report nausea, often describing it as a feeling of queasiness that may accompany other hepatic symptoms. Additionally, nausea has been observed following PTHC (parathyroid hormone therapy or a related procedure), suggesting that certain medical treatments can trigger the symptom.

Overall, nausea is a multifaceted symptom that can arise from pharmacological, dietary, psychological, infectious, and procedural origins, and it is typically managed with antiemetic therapy when it becomes clinically significant.&lt;SEP&gt;Nausea is a common gastrointestinal side effect associated with protease inhibitor therapy.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6c5e751c6db20a49f7ca0a341eadc654&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4b14c26a47ca283ef7a5696f26a6c99c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97c12585544967d7c896d91a36a80447&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-79f9159512d902b809ff6ebeca7cf146&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aecb82b4c17b2ce7e9445ff42d2200be&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8edba73be60673b1668775ee43b5d8dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8189651eadb45a933adca499117c49ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a175dfbbb5bdb8e9c075eb55543a5a41&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3978e5151245a8ce567b224992eabb69</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786424776</data>
</node>
<node id="Vomiting">
  <data key="d0">Vomiting</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Vomiting is the act of expelling the contents of the stomach through the mouth and is a physiological response that antiemetic medications are designed to prevent. It is regarded as a somatic symptom of anxiety, reflecting the body’s stress‑related response that can trigger the forceful ejection of gastric material. Various medical conditions may also manifest vomiting as a clinical sign: internal bleeding (hemorrhage) can provoke it, and it is frequently reported by individuals infected with hepatitis B. In autoimmune hepatitis, vomiting often accompanies nausea as part of the disease’s symptom complex.  

In clinical monitoring, vomiting is noted as an important post‑procedure observation following PTHC (post‑treatment health check), where its presence may indicate complications or the need for intervention. Additionally, certain dermatologic reactions—especially rashes associated with systemic involvement—can be accompanied by vomiting, underscoring its role as a non‑specific but valuable indicator of broader systemic disturbances.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6c5e751c6db20a49f7ca0a341eadc654&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-79f9159512d902b809ff6ebeca7cf146&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f45f05d7c26e4d3fdd9cc182e8964493&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aecb82b4c17b2ce7e9445ff42d2200be&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8edba73be60673b1668775ee43b5d8dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8189651eadb45a933adca499117c49ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27addf854956380fbaa2c0b20a96027b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3978e5151245a8ce567b224992eabb69</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786426870</data>
</node>
