<node id="Ophthalmological System">
  <data key="d0">Ophthalmological System</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The ophthalmological system is a body system that governs the structure and function of the eyes. It is categorized under the broader class of Body Systems and is central to visual perception, ocular health, and related physiological processes.

Because of its role in eye function, the ophthalmological system appears in a variety of clinical and research contexts. It is noted as being affected by antinausea drugs, suggesting that such medications can have ocular side effects. The system is also linked to insomnia, indicating that sleep disturbances may influence or be influenced by eye‑related mechanisms. Additionally, it is mentioned among body systems associated with personality disorders, reflecting investigations into possible connections between ocular biomarkers and psychiatric conditions.

In the field of pharmacogenetics, the ophthalmological system is specifically referenced in relation to eye health, highlighting the importance of genetic variation in drug response for ocular therapies. Phototherapy is another modality cited as having an impact on the system, implying that light‑based treatments can affect ocular tissues. Cosmetic procedures that involve the eyes also list the ophthalmological system, underscoring its relevance in aesthetic interventions.

Finally, the ophthalmological system is implicated in the pathology of Pseudoxanthoma Elasticum, a genetic disorder in which elastic fibers within ocular structures become calcified. This detail illustrates a concrete disease‑specific manifestation of the system’s involvement in broader connective‑tissue abnormalities.&lt;SEP&gt;Ophthalmological System is a body system evaluated by reflex tests.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-22d527eab17c7341eca433d361df1f72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-75141ec103928f9f43bbc8f2d882b9eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9102756028539b9228bf8362c3e96772&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e6d5f82c915dbc402d1fdeb23f98439f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5972068248f50904396788f92782210f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a63b2129b947c78015eda0d29076ec61&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-534d6ba3b100e1635acf1b4b824578b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ebfd4a7217e0e17b00a9c2caf49c2826&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b20a7bdac3dcdeae955d7f5fcff8809</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786427079</data>
</node>
<node id="Anti-inflammatory diets">
  <data key="d0">Anti-inflammatory diets</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Anti-inflammatory diets are dietary approaches used to prevent or treat inflammation, encompassing strategies to reduce acute and chronic inflammatory responses.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-97c12585544967d7c896d91a36a80447</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786386418</data>
</node>
<node id="Acute Inflammation">
  <data key="d0">Acute Inflammation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Acute inflammation is a normal, short-term immune response to injuries such as wounds, broken bones, or bee stings, facilitating healing.&lt;SEP&gt;Acute inflammation, also called pneumonitis, results from the antigen‑antibody reaction in hypersensitivity pneumonitis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6007bd886198fad2e92df82e7baf0b56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97c12585544967d7c896d91a36a80447</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786410477</data>
</node>
<node id="Chronic Inflammation">
  <data key="d0">Chronic Inflammation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Chronic inflammation, also called low-grade or systemic inflammation, is a prolonged immune reaction to persistent irritants and is linked to chronic disease.&lt;SEP&gt;Chronic inflammation is</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5abd633d480648e1041ecccc0a366802&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97c12585544967d7c896d91a36a80447</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786420337</data>
</node>
