<node id="Stomach">
  <data key="d0">Stomach</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Stomach is a hollow, muscular organ of the gastrointestinal system that functions primarily as a reservoir and initial site of digestion for ingested food. It secretes gastric acid and digestive enzymes, creating an acidic environment that breaks down food particles and prepares them for further processing in the small intestine. The acid produced by the stomach can act as a chemical irritant; if it leaks into the abdominal cavity it may contribute to conditions such as peritonitis.

The organ is susceptible to several pathological conditions. Ulcers commonly develop in the stomach lining, reflecting damage from excess acid or infection. A narrowing of the pyloric channel, known as pyloric stenosis, can obstruct the passage of stomach contents into the small intestine. In addition, the stomach may herniate through the diaphragmatic opening, leading to a hiatal hernia that allows part of the organ to enter the chest cavity. Blood originating from the stomach can also appear in diagnostic tests for hemoptysis, indicating that the organ can be a source of occult gastrointestinal bleeding.

Pharmacologically, antispasmodic drugs are used to alleviate painful stomach cramps by reducing smooth‑muscle contractions. Overall, the stomach’s role in storage, mechanical mixing, and chemical digestion is central to the digestive process, while its pathology and therapeutic considerations are important aspects of gastrointestinal health.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-44964dabcfecef02fb872f75721fb1f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4bcaad1542ea8e041fd9f427e13e25c0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5bc0e87b467a3072004e86dd7a21ec8c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2056dc204f71f79fef28e7940fdf482f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-de288167dfc93e3a334ec826d0c43f8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a3ecc34ed1c5383ab2125e8cf323393&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7db5d994bc1563d4fe5ab322fe7f3808&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0747cef262212b19792ffedcc3323ca</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786426614</data>
</node>
