</node>
<node id="Recipient">
  <data key="d0">Recipient</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Recipient is the individual who receives a donor organ, tissue, blood, or bone marrow and may be targeted by donor immune cells.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-07cc217c989e2af1a805d2ee5017b0b3</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786401939</data>
</node>
<node id="Donor">
  <data key="d0">Donor</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Donor is the individual who provides an organ, tissue, blood, or bone marrow containing immune cells that can trigger graft-versus-host disease.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-07cc217c989e2af1a805d2ee5017b0b3</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786401939</data>
</node>
<node id="Immunogenic Cells">
  <data key="d0">Immunogenic Cells</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Immunogenic Cells are donor immune cells that can attack the recipient and trigger graft-versus-host disease.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-07cc217c989e2af1a805d2ee5017b0b3</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786401940</data>
</node>
<node id="Cortisone-Like Drugs">
  <data key="d0">Cortisone-Like Drugs</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Cortisone-Like Drugs are pharmacological agents used to treat acute and chronic graft-versus-host disease.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-07cc217c989e2af1a805d2ee5017b0b3</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786401940</data>
</node>
<node id="Immunosuppressive Agents">
  <data key="d0">Immunosuppressive Agents</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Immunosuppressive Agents are drugs, such as cyclosporine, employed to suppress immune reactions in graft-versus-host disease.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-07cc217c989e2af1a805d2ee5017b0b3</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786401940</data>
</node>
<node id="Cyclosporine">
  <data key="d0">Cyclosporine</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Cyclosporine is an immunosuppressive drug used to treat graft-versus-host disease.&lt;SEP&gt;Cyclosporine is an immunosuppressant drug that inhibits T-cell activation, preventing transplanted organ attack.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-aeb4dffb5ad505f4839faaa747910b3c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-07cc217c989e2af1a805d2ee5017b0b3</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786411697</data>
</node>
<node id="Acute Leukemia">
  <data key="d0">Acute Leukemia</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Acute Leukemia is a fast‑progressing blood cancer mentioned in relation to bone marrow transplantation outcomes.&lt;SEP&gt;A type of leukemia that may require radiation therapy.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-07cc217c989e2af1a805d2ee5017b0b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ebc37c967098587c747ad0f9bafa5164</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786419286</data>
</node>
<node id="Survival Rates">
  <data key="d0">Survival Rates</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Survival Rates are statistical outcomes that have increased by 15–50 % following bone marrow transplantation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-07cc217c989e2af1a805d2ee5017b0b3</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786401941</data>
</node>
<node id="Testing">
  <data key="d0">Testing</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Testing is a laboratory method used to find donor‑recipient matches for transplantation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-07cc217c989e2af1a805d2ee5017b0b3</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786401941</data>
</node>
<node id="Leukemia Recurrence">
  <data key="d0">Leukemia Recurrence</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Leukemia Recurrence denotes the return of leukemia after treatment, a risk reduced in patients who develop graft-versus-host disease.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-07cc217c989e2af1a805d2ee5017b0b3</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786401941</data>
</node>
<node id="Glycosylated Hemoglobin Test">
  <data key="d0">Glycosylated Hemoglobin Test</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A diagnostic test that measures the percentage of hemoglobin bound to glucose, reflecting average blood sugar over the past two to four months and monitoring diabetes treatment effectiveness.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-96678691a6b9b46cb9e089fa58def217</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786401985</data>
</node>
<node id="Diabetes Mellitus">
  <data key="d0">Diabetes Mellitus</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A disease in which a person cannot effectively use blood sugar, leading to high glucose levels and increased risk of damage to eyes, kidneys, heart, nerves, blood vessels, and other organs.&lt;SEP&gt;Diabetes mellitus is listed as a key term associated with hemochromatosis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-96678691a6b9b46cb9e089fa58def217&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d78fea89c4fad7d739948de01e91d4a</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786404755</data>
</node>
<node id="Diabetes Control and Complications Trial (DCCT)">
  <data key="d0">Diabetes Control and Complications Trial (DCCT)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A clinical trial that demonstrated the relationship between glycated hemoglobin levels and outcomes in people with diabetes.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-96678691a6b9b46cb9e089fa58def217</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786401985</data>
</node>
<node id="Hemoglobin">
  <data key="d0">Hemoglobin</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">A protein found in every red blood cell that can bind glucose through the process of glycosylation.&lt;SEP&gt;The oxygen-carrying protein in red blood cells; released when red cells are destroyed.&lt;SEP&gt;Hemoglobin is a protein mentioned as the target of disorders such as sickle cell anemia and thalassemia.&lt;SEP&gt;Hemoglobin is a protein inside red blood cells that transports oxygen throughout the body.&lt;SEP&gt;Hemoglobin is a protein in red blood cells comprised of several types such as A1, A2, F, S, and C.&lt;SEP&gt;Oxygen-carrying protein in red blood cells composed of polypeptide chains and heme groups.&lt;SEP&gt;Hemoglobin is the oxygen‑carrying protein in red blood cells that contains heme as its iron‑containing portion.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2835448bb481a076c03436c11f787066&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-19e4810245f812dff5d4b92e29c6f847&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-556598665bad24192bd85d9014d7ac2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6dcc52e74701dc594a5093895fbb8763&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96678691a6b9b46cb9e089fa58def217&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7bace5728e2cd20cde6babfc53bd224f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-80e03e36082a52818b6740d8905c8a42</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786422919</data>
</node>
<node id="Red Blood Cell">
  <data key="d0">Red Blood Cell</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Red blood cells are the cells in blood that contain hemoglobin and have a lifespan of about 120 days.&lt;SEP&gt;Blood cells that contain hemoglobin and transport oxygen; when destroyed release hemoglobin.&lt;SEP&gt;Cell that carries oxygen to tissues.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ebc37c967098587c747ad0f9bafa5164&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96678691a6b9b46cb9e089fa58def217&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6dcc52e74701dc594a5093895fbb8763</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786419285</data>
</node>
<node id="A1c Hemoglobin">
  <data key="d0">A1c Hemoglobin</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A1c is the glycated form of hemoglobin formed when glucose binds to hemoglobin in red blood cells.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-96678691a6b9b46cb9e089fa58def217</data>
