  <data key="d0">Uterus</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Uterus – The uterus is the primary female reproductive organ located in the pelvis, encompassing a muscular cavity that connects superiorly to the fallopian tubes and inferiorly to the cervix. It serves as the site where a fertilized embryo implants during natural conception and assisted reproductive techniques such as in vitro fertilization (IVF). During pregnancy the uterus enlarges dramatically to accommodate the developing fetus, a process that can cause the organ to press on adjacent structures such as the ureters. The removal of the uterus (hysterectomy) has significant implications for hormone replacement therapy (HRT) regimens because the organ contributes to endocrine feedback loops.

The uterine cavity is routinely examined with specialized imaging and endoscopic procedures. Hysterosalpingography uses radiopaque contrast to assess the shape of the cavity and the patency of the fallopian tubes, while hysteroscopy allows direct visual inspection; during the latter the cavity may be distended with gas or fluid to improve visualization. Clinically, the uterus is the most commonly infected site in puerperal (post‑childbirth) infections, and it is also the typical location for benign growths such as fibroid (leiomyoma) tumors and the ectopic endometrial tissue characteristic of endometriosis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-929ba0da65b01f36a714e919c35cad8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be7a1c4e1372979af71be73cfdd576ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1089fcdf26e7799dcc1972363c2c7c82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1cc9b041882b1ffceb3664e339701aa0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a51c332fdfaf8c96be6ab58c809b221&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f9caaa60cd1726424ba3202fd3c2999a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ed0aad3bf0cbd3eab10e7535f31d9a6e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a6564f0ba00e16734a472181bcd545e9</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786426148</data>
</node>
