<node id="Hydrocelectomy">
  <data key="d0">Hydrocelectomy</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Hydrocelectomy is a surgical procedure that removes a hydrocele to prevent its recurrence.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a70a3ab31498f37b121780c8c0792259</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786409817</data>
</node>
<node id="Hydrocele">
  <data key="d0">Hydrocele</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Hydrocele is a fluid‑filled sac along the spermatic cord within a testicle, formed by peritoneal fluid backup in the tunica vaginalis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a70a3ab31498f37b121780c8c0792259</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786409817</data>
</node>
<node id="Spermatic Cord">
  <data key="d0">Spermatic Cord</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">The spermatic cord is the anatomical structure within the testicle along which a hydrocele can develop.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a70a3ab31498f37b121780c8c0792259</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786409817</data>
</node>
<node id="Testicle">
  <data key="d0">Testicle</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">The testicle is the male reproductive organ that contains the spermatic cord and may house a hydrocele.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a70a3ab31498f37b121780c8c0792259</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786409817</data>
</node>
<node id="Peritoneal Fluid">
  <data key="d0">Peritoneal Fluid</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Peritoneal fluid is abdominal fluid that can back up into the tunica vaginalis, contributing to hydrocele formation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a70a3ab31498f37b121780c8c0792259</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786409817</data>
</node>
<node id="Tunica Vaginalis">
  <data key="d0">Tunica Vaginalis</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">The tunica vaginalis is a membrane covering the front and sides of the male testes and can contain fluid in a hydrocele.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a70a3ab31498f37b121780c8c0792259</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786409817</data>
</node>
<node id="Processus Vaginalis">
  <data key="d0">Processus Vaginalis</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">The processus vaginalis is a portion of the testicular membrane that normally closes; failure to close can cause a congenital hydrocele.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a70a3ab31498f37b121780c8c0792259</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786409817</data>
</node>
<node id="Scrotum">
  <data key="d0">Scrotum</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">The scrotum is the external sac into which the testicles descend and where fluid may become trapped in a hydrocele.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a70a3ab31498f37b121780c8c0792259</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786409817</data>
</node>
<node id="Congenital Hydrocele">
  <data key="d0">Congenital Hydrocele</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Congenital hydrocele is a hydrocele present at birth caused by failure of the processus vaginalis to close.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a70a3ab31498f37b121780c8c0792259</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786409818</data>
</node>
<node id="Inguinal Hernia">
  <data key="d0">Inguinal Hernia</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Inguinal hernia is a medical condition listed as a key term related to the inguinal region.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a70a3ab31498f37b121780c8c0792259</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786409818</data>
</node>
<node id="Epididymis">
  <data key="d0">Epididymis</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">The epididymis is a coiled tube attached to the testicle involved in sperm transport.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a70a3ab31498f37b121780c8c0792259</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786409818</data>
</node>
<node id="Epididymitis">
  <data key="d0">Epididymitis</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Epididymitis is an inflammation of the epididymis, listed as a key term in the context of hydrocele discussion.&lt;SEP&gt;Epididymitis is inflammation of the epididymis and a risk factor for prostatitis.</data>
