  <data key="d1">UNKNOWN</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786410072</data>
</node>
<node id="Hypercalcemia">
  <data key="d0">Hypercalcemia</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Hypercalcemia is a disease/condition characterized by an abnormally high blood calcium level, usually above 10.5 mg/dL.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ccd7967460bd192566c346ccf6adf5af</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786410111</data>
</node>
<node id="Primary Hyperparathyroidism">
  <data key="d0">Primary Hyperparathyroidism</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Primary hyperparathyroidism is the excessive secretion of parathyroid hormone by parathyroid glands, identified as the most common cause of hypercalcemia.&lt;SEP&gt;Primary hyperparathyroidism is a condition caused by an adenoma of the parathyroid gland, leading to elevated calcium levels.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ccd7967460bd192566c346ccf6adf5af&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f08a91dc11110c75553c2f3174360dde</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786410138</data>
</node>
<node id="Parathyroid Hormone">
  <data key="d0">Parathyroid Hormone</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Parathyroid hormone (PTH) is a hormone measured in blood to diagnose hypercalcemia and primary hyperparathyroidism.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ccd7967460bd192566c346ccf6adf5af</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786410111</data>
</node>
<node id="Parathyroid Glands">
  <data key="d0">Parathyroid Glands</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Parathyroid glands are endocrine organs that secrete parathyroid hormone and can be sources of primary hyperparathyroidism.&lt;SEP&gt;The parathyroid glands, attached to the back of the thyroid, regulate calcium balance in the body.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ccd7967460bd192566c346ccf6adf5af&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-01a42939570e7d067a66881fea563910</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786422228</data>
</node>
<node id="Cancer">
  <data key="d0">Cancer</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Cancer is a broad disease category that denotes any malignant condition characterized by uncontrolled cell growth and the potential to invade surrounding tissues and metastasize to distant sites.  Malignancies classified under the term cancer can affect virtually any organ system, including the colon, rectum, prostate, skin, and intestines, and each subtype may present with distinct clinical features and diagnostic findings.  

In the gastrointestinal tract, malignant tumors such as colon, rectal, or intestinal cancers can cause intestinal obstruction, which worsens prognosis by limiting nutrient absorption and precipitating emergency surgical interventions.  Prostate cancer, a specific form of the disease, commonly appears on transrectal ultrasound as an irregular hypoechoic area, aiding radiologic detection and guiding biopsies.  

Cutaneous malignancies, particularly those accelerated by prolonged ultraviolet exposure, are also encompassed by the term cancer.  Individuals with photosensitivity are at heightened risk for developing malignant skin tumors, underscoring the role of environmental factors in tumorigenesis.  

Systemically, cancer can produce metabolic disturbances such as hypercalcemia, a condition in which elevated calcium levels arise from tumor‑derived factors or bone resorption.  Additionally, many cancers induce a state of nondeliberate immunosuppression, weakening the host’s natural defenses and increasing susceptibility to infections.  Because of this immune dysfunction, immunologic therapies—such as checkpoint inhibitors, CAR‑T cells, and cancer vaccines—are employed to boost the immune system’s capacity to recognize and eradicate malignant cells.  

Overall, cancer represents a heterogeneous group of malignant diseases that share the common hallmark of uncontrolled cellular proliferation, yet differ widely in anatomical origin, clinical presentation, associated complications, and therapeutic approaches.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-870bb1b70305260567e1654eccb86793&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4746af750ee6290790a52a00be9ec1c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b83572ef3bc13b6de1b3591b9a778c3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b29429d857edf1192105634eda51b928&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ccd7967460bd192566c346ccf6adf5af&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ebc37c967098587c747ad0f9bafa5164&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1ccba52c964c301daf559927d7d7c5e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-09934893a08d3cab96095297ececeeb7</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786426777</data>
</node>
<node id="HBOT">
  <data key="d0">HBOT</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Hyperbaric oxygen therapy (HBOT) is a treatment mentioned as sometimes failing to improve hypercalcemia or causing complications.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ccd7967460bd192566c346ccf6adf5af</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786410111</data>
</node>
<node id="Acute Hemodialysis">
  <data key="d0">Acute Hemodialysis</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Acute hemodialysis is a medical procedure used to safely reduce dangerous calcium levels in patients with hypercalcemia and impaired kidney function.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ccd7967460bd192566c346ccf6adf5af</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786410111</data>
</node>
<node id="Kidney Function">
  <data key="d0">Kidney Function</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Kidney function determines suitability of intravenous fluid therapy versus hemodialysis in hypercalcemia management.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ccd7967460bd192566c346ccf6adf5af</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786410112</data>
</node>
<node id="Serum Albumin">
  <data key="d0">Serum Albumin</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Serum albumin is a blood protein measured during diagnosis of hypercalcemia.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ccd7967460bd192566c346ccf6adf5af</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786410112</data>
</node>
<node id="Phosphate">
  <data key="d0">Phosphate</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Phosphate is a blood mineral whose lower levels, together with higher calcium, may suggest primary hyperparathyroidism.</data>
