  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Thiazide is a class of diuretic drugs that can cause hypokalemia.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f8a0239df88721094e3c51f8a7373134</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786410760</data>
</node>
<node id="Congestive Heart Failure">
  <data key="d0">Congestive Heart Failure</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Congestive heart failure is a condition involving fluid buildup in the body, treated in part with diuretics.&lt;SEP&gt;Congestive Heart Failure is a cardiovascular condition that can cause transudative pleural effusion and bilateral fluid accumulation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-448825a5baf21347c5a8fd84c362669b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8a0239df88721094e3c51f8a7373134</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786422150</data>
</node>
<node id="Cardiac Monitor">
  <data key="d0">Cardiac Monitor</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Cardiac monitor is a medical device used to continuously monitor heart activity in severe hypokalemia cases.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f8a0239df88721094e3c51f8a7373134</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786410760</data>
</node>
<node id="Laxative Abuse">
  <data key="d0">Laxative Abuse</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Laxative abuse is the chronic misuse of laxatives, which can cause hypokalemia, especially in individuals with eating disorders.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f8a0239df88721094e3c51f8a7373134</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786410760</data>
</node>
<node id="American Dietetic Association">
  <data key="d0">American Dietetic Association</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">American Dietetic Association is a professional organization that recommends dietary strategies, such as a high potassium diet, for patients on diuretics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f8a0239df88721094e3c51f8a7373134</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786410761</data>
</node>
<node id="High Potassium Diet">
  <data key="d0">High Potassium Diet</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">High potassium diet is a nutritional approach rich in potassium foods, recommended to prevent or treat hypokalemia.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f8a0239df88721094e3c51f8a7373134</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786410761</data>
</node>
<node id="Ileus">
  <data key="d0">Ileus</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Ileus is a disruption of normal intestinal motility that can be associated with hypokalemia.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f8a0239df88721094e3c51f8a7373134</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786410761</data>
</node>
<node id="High potassium diet">
  <data key="d0">High potassium diet</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f8a0239df88721094e3c51f8a7373134</data>
  <data key="d2">The American Dietetic Association recommends a high potassium diet for patients taking diuretics.</data>
  <data key="d1">UNKNOWN</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786410762</data>
</node>
<node id="Hypoparathyroidism">
  <data key="d0">Hypoparathyroidism</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">... (so included)</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-122326791c5aa6c1ab51dcc4808a72bc</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786410802</data>
</node>
<node id="Hypopituitarism">
  <data key="d0">Hypopituitarism</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Hypopituitarism is a disease characterized by loss of function in endocrine glands due to failure of the pituitary gland to secrete stimulating hormones.&lt;SEP&gt;Hypopituitarism is a permanent endocrine disorder mentioned in the prognosis section with an overall excellent prognosis if treated.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eee26fc83e6a72a8d68824995539e61e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-14cced56b63272cdcfcc286787f3aa8d</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786410917</data>
</node>
<node id="Anterior Pituitary">
  <data key="d0">Anterior Pituitary</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">The anterior pituitary is the front portion of the pituitary gland that produces its own hormones for endocrine regulation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eee26fc83e6a72a8d68824995539e61e</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786410820</data>
</node>
<node id="Posterior Pituitary">
  <data key="d0">Posterior Pituitary</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">The posterior pituitary is the rear portion of the pituitary gland that stores and releases hormones from the hypothalamus.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eee26fc83e6a72a8d68824995539e61e</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786410820</data>
</node>
<node id="Pituitary Stalk">
  <data key="d0">Pituitary Stalk</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">The pituitary stalk is the connection between the hypothalamus and the pituitary gland that can be damaged by injury or surgery, leading to hypopituitarism.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eee26fc83e6a72a8d68824995539e61e</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786410820</data>
</node>
<node id="Gonadotropin">
  <data key="d0">Gonadotropin</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Gonadotropin is a class of hormones that includes luteinizing hormone and follicle‑stimulating hormone, regulating gonadal function.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eee26fc83e6a72a8d68824995539e61e</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786410820</data>
</node>
<node id="Luteinizing Hormone (LH)">
  <data key="d0">Luteinizing Hormone (LH)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Luteinizing hormone is a specific gonadotropin measured in blood tests to assess pituitary function.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eee26fc83e6a72a8d68824995539e61e</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786410820</data>
</node>
<node id="Follicle‑Stimulating Hormone (FSH)">
  <data key="d0">Follicle‑Stimulating Hormone (FSH)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Follicle‑stimulating hormone is a specific gonadotropin measured in blood tests to assess pituitary function.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eee26fc83e6a72a8d68824995539e61e</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786410820</data>
</node>
<node id="Gonadal Steroid Levels">
  <data key="d0">Gonadal Steroid Levels</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Gonadal steroid levels are hormone concentrations produced by the gonads, evaluated alongside LH and FSH to diagnose hormone deficiencies.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eee26fc83e6a72a8d68824995539e61e</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786410820</data>
</node>
<node id="Growth Hormone">
  <data key="d0">Growth Hormone</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Growth hormone is a pituitary hormone that stimulates growth and metabolism, whose deficiency is assessed in hypopituitarism.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eee26fc83e6a72a8d68824995539e61e</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786410820</data>
</node>
<node id="Hypospadias">
  <data key="d0">Hypospadias</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Hypospadias is a congenital defect in males where the urethra opens on the underside of the penis, and is one of the most common congenital abnormalities in the United States.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-14cced56b63272cdcfcc286787f3aa8d</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786410916</data>
</node>
<node id="Epispadias">
  <data key="d0">Epispadias</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Epispadias, also called bladder exstrophy, is a congenital defect in males where the urethra opens on the upper surface of the penis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-14cced56b63272cdcfcc286787f3aa8d</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786410916</data>
</node>
<node id="Bladder Exstrophy">
  <data key="d0">Bladder Exstrophy</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Bladder exstrophy is an alternative name for epispadias, a congenital defect with the urethra opening on the dorsum of the penis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-14cced56b63272cdcfcc286787f3aa8d</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786410916</data>
</node>
<node id="Penis">
  <data key="d0">Penis</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">The penis is the male external sexual organ where hypospadias and epispadias affect the position of the urethral opening.&lt;SEP&gt;The penis is the male sexual organ whose blood flow and nerve function are involved in achieving erection, central to impotence.&lt;SEP&gt;external male organ examined for lumps, abnormalities; site of malignant cells.&lt;SEP&gt;The penis is the external male organ located anterior to the prostate.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1d92409f9e3722b81f594b7b8d561f3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2c9e6467acd0c81d0621428aab938849&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-abbfd6cbf4afd6662ba2707fbc17c147&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-14cced56b63272cdcfcc286787f3aa8d</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786424847</data>
</node>
<node id="Male">
  <data key="d0">Male</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Male refers to the biological sex in which hypospadias and epispadias primarily occur.&lt;SEP&gt;Male refers to the sex in which the bladder and prostate are positioned anterior to the rectum.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1ccba52c964c301daf559927d7d7c5e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-14cced56b63272cdcfcc286787f3aa8d</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786426767</data>
</node>
<node id="Female">
  <data key="d0">Female</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Female refers to the biological sex in which a rare form of hypospadias can open the urethra into the vagina.&lt;SEP&gt;Female refers to the sex in which the vagina is positioned anterior to the rectum.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1ccba52c964c301daf559927d7d7c5e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-14cced56b63272cdcfcc286787f3aa8d</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786426767</data>
</node>
<node id="Psychological Trauma">
  <data key="d0">Psychological Trauma</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Psychological trauma is a potential negative impact that early surgical repair aims to minimize.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-14cced56b63272cdcfcc286787f3aa8d</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786410917</data>
</node>
<node id="Cengage Learning">
  <data key="d0">Cengage Learning</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Cengage Learning is the publisher credited for an illustration of hypospadias.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-14cced56b63272cdcfcc286787f3aa8d</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786410917</data>
</node>
<node id="Argosy Inc.">
  <data key="d0">Argosy Inc.</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Argosy Inc. is the illustration contributor credited in the text.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-14cced56b63272cdcfcc286787f3aa8d</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786410917</data>
</node>
<node id="Continence">
  <data key="d0">Continence</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Continence is a key term related to urinary function mentioned in the entry.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-14cced56b63272cdcfcc286787f3aa8d</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786410917</data>
</node>
<node id="Renal">
  <data key="d0">Renal</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Renal denotes the body system category listed for the disorder.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-14cced56b63272cdcfcc286787f3aa8d</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786410917</data>
</node>
<node id="Liebmann">
  <data key="d0">Liebmann</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Liebmann is a name listed among key terms in the entry, likely an author or contributor.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-14cced56b63272cdcfcc286787f3aa8d</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786410917</data>
</node>
<node id="Clitoris">
  <data key="d0">Clitoris</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">The clitoris is the female anatomical structure located near the urethral opening, mentioned as a reference point for the female defect location.&lt;SEP&gt;The highly innervated erectile tissue of the female genitalia inspected during a pelvic exam.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-506592426d0a0ade6d5d62f2ac15f607&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-14cced56b63272cdcfcc286787f3aa8d</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786419210</data>
</node>
<node id="Penile Shaft">
  <data key="d0">Penile Shaft</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">The penile shaft is the part of the penis along which the urethra opens in hypospadias.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-14cced56b63272cdcfcc286787f3aa8d</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786410917</data>
</node>
<node id="Penile Tip">
  <data key="d0">Penile Tip</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">The penile tip is the distal end of the penis where the urethral opening normally resides, contrasted with hypospadias placement.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-14cced56b63272cdcfcc286787f3aa8d</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786410917</data>
</node>
<node id="Hypotension">
  <data key="d0">Hypotension</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Hypotension is a medical condition mentioned in the causes section of the entry.&lt;SEP&gt;Hypotension is the medical condition characterized by low blood pressure.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6a3b3c33dc539d83724153cf8bd92691&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-14cced56b63272cdcfcc286787f3aa8d</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786410947</data>
</node>
<node id="Postural Hypotension">
  <data key="d0">Postural Hypotension</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Postural hypotension is a common form of low blood pressure that occurs after quickly sitting up or standing.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6a3b3c33dc539d83724153cf8bd92691</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786410947</data>
</node>
<node id="Diabetes">
  <data key="d0">Diabetes</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Diabetes can cause nerve damage that disrupts blood‑pressure reflexes, leading to postural hypotension.&lt;SEP&gt;Diabetes is a disease that can contribute to acquired ptosis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-bf2a4a7521a933797a5c97cd08b21a3d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a3b3c33dc539d83724153cf8bd92691</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786425872</data>
</node>
<node id="Medication Adjustment">
  <data key="d0">Medication Adjustment</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Medication adjustment involves changing drugs to prevent or treat postural hypotension.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6a3b3c33dc539d83724153cf8bd92691</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786410947</data>
</node>
<node id="Gradual Position Change">
  <data key="d0">Gradual Position Change</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Gradual position change means rising slowly or getting out of bed in stages to reduce symptoms of postural hypotension.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6a3b3c33dc539d83724153cf8bd92691</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786410947</data>
</node>
<node id="Muscles">
  <data key="d0">Muscles</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Muscles influence blood pressure during activities such as exercise and stress.&lt;SEP&gt;Muscles are body tissues that can grow in size and strength to compensate for paralyzed muscles in Postpolio Syndrome.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c71b04609b9999bd4f4017776dc567f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a3b3c33dc539d83724153cf8bd92691</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786423103</data>
</node>
<node id="Pap Smear">
  <data key="d0">Pap Smear</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Pap smear is a screening method related to cervical health and listed among key terms.&lt;SEP&gt;A cytological test performed during a pelvic exam to detect abnormal cells in the cervix.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-07a9a426a4bb2afd30f9a958bbb935d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-506592426d0a0ade6d5d62f2ac15f607</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786419211</data>
</node>
<node id="Prolapsed Uterus">
  <data key="d0">Prolapsed Uterus</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Prolapsed uterus is a medical condition noted as a key term in the hysterectomy entry.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-07a9a426a4bb2afd30f9a958bbb935d6</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786410983</data>
</node>
<node id="Womens General Health">
  <data key="d0">Womens General Health</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Women’s general health is an area of expertise for gynecologists as described in the entry.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-07a9a426a4bb2afd30f9a958bbb935d6</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786410983</data>
</node>
<node id="Labor And Childbirth">
  <data key="d0">Labor And Childbirth</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Labor and childbirth is an area of expertise for gynecologists as described in the entry.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-07a9a426a4bb2afd30f9a958bbb935d6</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786410983</data>
</node>
<node id="Genetics">
  <data key="d0">Genetics</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Genetics is an area of expertise for gynecologists as described in the entry.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetics is being studied...</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9afe775e3de6a2ede3813b294c2d7085&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-07a9a426a4bb2afd30f9a958bbb935d6</data>
