</node>
<node id="Pneumonectomy">
  <data key="d0">Pneumonectomy</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Pneumonectomy is a surgical procedure that removes a lung, used primarily to treat lung cancer.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f81367bc23721681b080e8e55f88ab71</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786422128</data>
</node>
<node id="Lung Cancer">
  <data key="d0">Lung Cancer</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Lung cancer is a leading cause of cancer-related deaths, strongly associated with cigarette smoking.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f81367bc23721681b080e8e55f88ab71</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786422128</data>
</node>
<node id="Conventional Pneumonectomy">
  <data key="d0">Conventional Pneumonectomy</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Conventional pneumonectomy is a type of pneumonectomy where only the diseased lung is removed.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f81367bc23721681b080e8e55f88ab71</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786422128</data>
</node>
<node id="Partial Pneumonectomy">
  <data key="d0">Partial Pneumonectomy</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Partial pneumonectomy is a type of pneumonectomy where one or more lobes of a lung are removed.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f81367bc23721681b080e8e55f88ab71</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786422128</data>
</node>
<node id="Extrapleural Pneumonectomy">
  <data key="d0">Extrapleural Pneumonectomy</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Extrapleural pneumonectomy is an extensive surgery removing the lung, pericaridium, part of the diaphragm, and the parietal pleura.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f81367bc23721681b080e8e55f88ab71</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786422129</data>
</node>
<node id="Pericaridium">
  <data key="d0">Pericaridium</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Pericaridium is the membrane covering the heart that may be removed during extrapleural pneumonectomy.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f81367bc23721681b080e8e55f88ab71</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786422129</data>
</node>
<node id="Parietal Pleura">
  <data key="d0">Parietal Pleura</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Parietal pleura is the membrane lining the chest cavity and may be removed in extrapleural pneumonectomy.&lt;SEP&gt;Parietal Pleura is the thin membrane that lines the inside of the chest wall.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-448825a5baf21347c5a8fd84c362669b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f81367bc23721681b080e8e55f88ab71</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786422149</data>
</node>
<node id="Intravenous Line">
  <data key="d0">Intravenous Line</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">An intravenous line is a medical device inserted into a vein to deliver fluids and medication throughout an operation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f81367bc23721681b080e8e55f88ab71</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786422129</data>
</node>
<node id="Respirator">
  <data key="d0">Respirator</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A respirator is a mechanical breathing support device that assists patients in maintaining respiration post‑operation.&lt;SEP&gt;A respirator is medical equipment that may be used to help patients with paralytic Polio breathe.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6a43117074cce1d59c31131832dfa007&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f81367bc23721681b080e8e55f88ab71</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786422232</data>
</node>
<node id="Surgical Intensive Care Unit">
  <data key="d0">Surgical Intensive Care Unit</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">The surgical intensive care unit is a specialized hospital area where patients recover immediately after major surgery.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f81367bc23721681b080e8e55f88ab71</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786422130</data>
</node>
<node id="Regular Hospital Room">
  <data key="d0">Regular Hospital Room</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A regular hospital room is a standard inpatient room where patients are transferred after leaving intensive care.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f81367bc23721681b080e8e55f88ab71</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786422130</data>
</node>
<node id="Cigarette Smoking">
  <data key="d0">Cigarette Smoking</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Cigarette smoking is a behavior that contributes to the development of lung cancer.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f81367bc23721681b080e8e55f88ab71</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786422130</data>
</node>
<node id="Lung Lobe">
  <data key="d0">Lung Lobe</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">A lung lobe is a subdivision of the lung that may be removed during partial pneumonectomy.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f81367bc23721681b080e8e55f88ab71</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786422130</data>
</node>
<node id="Pleural Effusion">
  <data key="d0">Pleural Effusion</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Pleural Effusion is a condition where excess fluid accumulates in the pleural space, leading to shortness of breath, chest pain, dyspepsia, and cough.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-448825a5baf21347c5a8fd84c362669b</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786422149</data>
</node>
<node id="Pleural Space">
  <data key="d0">Pleural Space</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Pleural Space is the anatomical cavity between the visceral pleura and the parietal pleura that normally contains a small amount of lubricating fluid.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-448825a5baf21347c5a8fd84c362669b</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786422149</data>
</node>
<node id="Visceral Pleura">
  <data key="d0">Visceral Pleura</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Visceral Pleura is the thin membrane that lines the surface of the lungs.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-448825a5baf21347c5a8fd84c362669b</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786422149</data>
</node>
<node id="Chest Wall">
  <data key="d0">Chest Wall</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Chest Wall is the outer structure of the thoracic cavity that encloses the lungs and pleural membranes.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-448825a5baf21347c5a8fd84c362669b</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786422149</data>
</node>
<node id="Pleural Fluid">
  <data key="d0">Pleural Fluid</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Pleural Fluid is the lubricating fluid normally produced by small blood vessels in the pleural linings.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-448825a5baf21347c5a8fd84c362669b</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786422149</data>
</node>
<node id="Transudative Pleural Effusion">
  <data key="d0">Transudative Pleural Effusion</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Transudative Pleural Effusion is a type of pleural effusion formed when fluid moves into the pleural space due to pressure imbalances, commonly caused by heart failure.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-448825a5baf21347c5a8fd84c362669b</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786422150</data>
</node>
<node id="Bilateral Effusion">
  <data key="d0">Bilateral Effusion</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Bilateral Effusion refers to pleural effusion present on both sides of the chest.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-448825a5baf21347c5a8fd84c362669b</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786422150</data>
</node>
<node id="Dyspepsia">
  <data key="d0">Dyspepsia</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Dyspepsia is gastric discomfort that can occur in pleural effusion.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-448825a5baf21347c5a8fd84c362669b</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786422150</data>
</node>
<node id="Ultrasound Scan">
  <data key="d0">Ultrasound Scan</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Ultrasound Scan is a diagnostic method that can reveal small pleural effusions not seen on examination.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-448825a5baf21347c5a8fd84c362669b</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786422151</data>
</node>
<node id="Lung Infection">
  <data key="d0">Lung Infection</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Lung Infection is a pathological condition of the lung tissue that can trigger the development of pleural effusion.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-448825a5baf21347c5a8fd84c362669b</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786422151</data>
</node>
<node id="Thoracentesis">
  <data key="d0">Thoracentesis</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Thoracentesis is a medical procedure that drains excess pleural fluid from the pleural space using a needle or catheter.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-448825a5baf21347c5a8fd84c362669b</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786422151</data>
</node>
<node id="Large-bore Needle">
  <data key="d0">Large-bore Needle</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Large-bore Needle is an instrument employed during thoracentesis to access and remove pleural fluid.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-448825a5baf21347c5a8fd84c362669b</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786422151</data>
</node>
<node id="Sclerosis">
  <data key="d0">Sclerosis</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Sclerosis refers to a fibrotic process that can seal the pleural space and prevent recurrent large effusions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-448825a5baf21347c5a8fd84c362669b</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786422151</data>
</node>
<node id="Primary Disease">
  <data key="d0">Primary Disease</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Primary Disease denotes the underlying condition (e.g., heart failure, infection) whose timely diagnosis and treatment can prevent pleural effusion.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-448825a5baf21347c5a8fd84c362669b</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786422151</data>
</node>
<node id="Exudate">
  <data key="d0">Exudate</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Exudate is a type of pleural fluid rich in proteins and cells, typically associated with infections or inflammation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-448825a5baf21347c5a8fd84c362669b</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786422151</data>
</node>
<node id="Transudate">
  <data key="d0">Transudate</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Transudate is a low‑protein pleural fluid resulting from pressure imbalances, commonly seen in heart failure‑related</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-448825a5baf21347c5a8fd84c362669b</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786422151</data>
</node>
<node id="Polyglandular Deficiency Syndromes">
  <data key="d0">Polyglandular Deficiency Syndromes</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Polyglandular deficiency syndromes are disorders characterized by failure of multiple endocrine glands to produce sufficient hormones.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-01a42939570e7d067a66881fea563910</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786422228</data>
</node>
<node id="Autoimmune Response">
  <data key="d0">Autoimmune Response</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">An autoimmune response is a condition in which the body generates antibodies that mistakenly target its own tissues.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-01a42939570e7d067a66881fea563910</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786422228</data>
</node>
<node id="Type I Polyglandular Deficiency Syndrome">
  <data key="d0">Type I Polyglandular Deficiency Syndrome</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Type I is a childhood-onset form of polyglandular deficiency characterized by failure of multiple endocrine glands and associated symptoms such as hepatitis, hair loss, and skin pigment changes.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-01a42939570e7d067a66881fea563910</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786422229</data>
</node>
<node id="Polio">
  <data key="d0">Polio</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Polio is an infectious disease caused by a virus that can lead to paralysis, respiratory failure, or death, and is classified as a disease/condition.&lt;SEP&gt;Polio is a disease caused by the poliovirus that primarily infects children and can cause paralysis of limbs and the respiratory system.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c71b04609b9999bd4f4017776dc567f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a43117074cce1d59c31131832dfa007</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786423103</data>
</node>
<node id="Poliomyelitis">
  <data key="d0">Poliomyelitis</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Poliomyelitis is another term for Polio, describing the infectious disease caused by the poliovirus.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6a43117074cce1d59c31131832dfa007</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786422231</data>
</node>
<node id="Poliovirus">
  <data key="d0">Poliovirus</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Poliovirus is the virus that causes Polio and normally resides in the human digestive tract.&lt;SEP&gt;Poliovirus is the virus that infects individuals through the mouth and leads to polio.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c71b04609b9999bd4f4017776dc567f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a43117074cce1d59c31131832dfa007</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786423103</data>
</node>
<node id="Enteroviruses">
  <data key="d0">Enteroviruses</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Enteroviruses are the viral family to which poliovirus belongs, infecting the gastrointestinal tract.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6a43117074cce1d59c31131832dfa007</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786422231</data>
</node>
<node id="Type 1 Poliovirus">
  <data key="d0">Type 1 Poliovirus</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Type 1 Poliovirus is a strain of poliovirus that is responsible for epidemics and the most severe manifestations of Polio.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6a43117074cce1d59c31131832dfa007</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786422231</data>
