</node>
<node id="Uremic Poisoning">
  <data key="d0">Uremic Poisoning</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Uremic Poisoning is a complication listed among key terms that can arise from impaired kidney function in Polycystic Kidney Disease.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d855036a34135250bf6b6942f08845d7</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786422503</data>
</node>
<node id="Oligohydramnios">
  <data key="d0">Oligohydramnios</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Oligohydramnios is a deficiency of amniotic fluid, classified as a severe disease/condition affecting the fetus.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9ec71b0a5c34e36d6cca673cad0a2921</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786422809</data>
</node>
<node id="Polyhydramnios">
  <data key="d0">Polyhydramnios</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Polyhydramnios is an excess of amniotic fluid, classified as a disease/condition that can be severe.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9ec71b0a5c34e36d6cca673cad0a2921</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786422809</data>
</node>
<node id="Amniotic Fluid Abnormalities">
  <data key="d0">Amniotic Fluid Abnormalities</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Amniotic fluid abnormalities refer to conditions of excess or deficiency of amniotic fluid, encompassing polyhydramnios and oligohydramnios.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9ec71b0a5c34e36d6cca673cad0a2921</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786422809</data>
</node>
<node id="Maternal Diabetes">
  <data key="d0">Maternal Diabetes</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Maternal diabetes is a maternal condition associated with an enlarged fetus and a common cause of polyhydramnios.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9ec71b0a5c34e36d6cca673cad0a2921</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786422809</data>
</node>
<node id="Twin Gestation">
  <data key="d0">Twin Gestation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Twin gestation is a pregnancy with multiple fetuses that is prone to polyhydramnios.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9ec71b0a5c34e36d6cca673cad0a2921</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786422809</data>
</node>
<node id="Preeclampsia">
  <data key="d0">Preeclampsia</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Preeclampsia is a pregnancy complication characterized by dangerously high blood pressure, swelling, and protein in the urine.&lt;SEP&gt;Preeclampsia is a condition mentioned as a key term related to prepregnancy counseling.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3cdce04fe6b7fc2f2383ff5401c7803f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0562388bfd043886184b76d26796a668</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786423559</data>
</node>
<node id="Eclampsia">
  <data key="d0">Eclampsia</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Eclampsia is a severe progression of preeclampsia that can occur if the syndrome is not treated.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3cdce04fe6b7fc2f2383ff5401c7803f</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786422886</data>
</node>
<node id="Protein in Urine">
  <data key="d0">Protein in Urine</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Protein in urine is a symptom of preeclampsia indicating renal involvement.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3cdce04fe6b7fc2f2383ff5401c7803f</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786422886</data>
</node>
<node id="Systolic Pressure">
  <data key="d0">Systolic Pressure</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Systolic pressure is the first (higher) number in a blood pressure reading representing pressure during heart contraction.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3cdce04fe6b7fc2f2383ff5401c7803f</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786422886</data>
</node>
<node id="Diastolic Pressure">
  <data key="d0">Diastolic Pressure</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Diastolic pressure is the second (lower) number in a blood pressure reading representing pressure when the heart relaxes.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3cdce04fe6b7fc2f2383ff5401c7803f</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786422887</data>
</node>
<node id="Millimeters Of Mercury">
  <data key="d0">Millimeters Of Mercury</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Millimeters of mercury (mm Hg) is the unit used to report blood pressure values.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3cdce04fe6b7fc2f2383ff5401c7803f</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786422887</data>
</node>
<node id="Tissues">
  <data key="d0">Tissues</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Tissues are groups of cells throughout the body that receive blood delivered by arteries.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3cdce04fe6b7fc2f2383ff5401c7803f</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786422887</data>
</node>
<node id="Stool">
  <data key="d0">Stool</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Stool is solid waste through which excess porphyrins are excreted in porphyria.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-556598665bad24192bd85d9014d7ac2a</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786422919</data>
</node>
<node id="Postpartum Depression">
  <data key="d0">Postpartum Depression</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Postpartum Depression is a severe mood disorder that begins after childbirth, lasting at least six weeks and affecting approximately 15% of childbearing women.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-32995d0e87b6ef1faa098707755b406f</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786422994</data>
</node>
<node id="Fathers">
  <data key="d0">Fathers</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Fathers may experience increased levels of depression associated with postpartum depression in the family.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-32995d0e87b6ef1faa098707755b406f</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786422994</data>
</node>
<node id="Other Children">
  <data key="d0">Other Children</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Other children in the family may be affected by a mother's postpartum depression.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-32995d0e87b6ef1faa098707755b406f</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786422994</data>
</node>
<node id="Partners">
  <data key="d0">Partners</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Partners (spouses or significant others) can be impacted by postpartum depression.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-32995d0e87b6ef1faa098707755b406f</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786422994</data>
</node>
<node id="Previous Depressive Episode">
  <data key="d0">Previous Depressive Episode</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A prior depressive episode not associated with pregnancy raises the risk of developing postpartum depression.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-32995d0e87b6ef1faa098707755b406f</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786422994</data>
</node>
<node id="Subsequent Pregnancy">
  <data key="d0">Subsequent Pregnancy</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">A later pregnancy after an episode of postpartum depression increases the risk of recurrence.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-32995d0e87b6ef1faa098707755b406f</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786422994</data>
</node>
<node id="Clinical Interview">
  <data key="d0">Clinical Interview</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Clinical interview is a diagnostic method involving a healthcare provider assessing symptoms of postpartum depression.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-32995d0e87b6ef1faa098707755b406f</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786422995</data>
</node>
<node id="Healthcare Provider">
  <data key="d0">Healthcare Provider</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Other professional healthcare providers may conduct diagnosis and treatment for postpartum depression.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-32995d0e87b6ef1faa098707755b406f</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786422995</data>
</node>
<node id="Antidepressant Medication">
  <data key="d0">Antidepressant Medication</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Antidepressant medication refers to drug classes prescribed to treat postpartum depression, often used for three to four weeks.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-32995d0e87b6ef1faa098707755b406f</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786422995</data>
</node>
<node id="Counseling">
  <data key="d0">Counseling</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Counseling is a therapeutic method offered as part of treatment for postpartum depression.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-32995d0e87b6ef1faa098707755b406f</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786422995</data>
</node>
<node id="Group Treatment">
  <data key="d0">Group Treatment</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Group treatment involves collective therapy sessions for individuals with postpartum depression.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-32995d0e87b6ef1faa098707755b406f</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786422995</data>
</node>
<node id="Support Strategies">
  <data key="d0">Support Strategies</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Support strategies are non‑medical approaches, such as peer support, used to aid recovery from postpartum depression.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-32995d0e87b6ef1faa098707755b406f</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786422995</data>
</node>
<node id="Portal Vein Bypass">
  <data key="d0">Portal Vein Bypass</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Portal vein bypass is listed as a disease/disorder entry with unknown severity, affecting the cardiovascular, gastrointestinal, renal, and hematological systems.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2c10b11558c17065dc27bd62ccb0fb84</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786423011</data>
</node>
<node id="Acute Porphyria">
  <data key="d0">Acute Porphyria</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Acute porphyria is a condition for which individuals with a family history should be screened, even if symptoms are absent.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2c10b11558c17065dc27bd62ccb0fb84</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786423011</data>
</node>
<node id="Congenital Erythropoietic Porphyria">
  <data key="d0">Congenital Erythropoietic Porphyria</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Congenital erythropoietic porphyria is a specific type of porphyria for which prenatal diagnosis has been successfully accomplished.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2c10b11558c17065dc27bd62ccb0fb84</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786423011</data>
</node>
<node id="Heart Monitor">
  <data key="d0">Heart Monitor</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A heart monitor is a device to which patients are connected to monitor vital functions after portal vein bypass.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2c10b11558c17065dc27bd62ccb0fb84</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786423011</data>
</node>
<node id="Nasogastric Tube">
  <data key="d0">Nasogastric Tube</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A nasogastric tube is used to feed patients after portal vein bypass.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2c10b11558c17065dc27bd62ccb0fb84</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786423011</data>
</node>
<node id="Positron Emission Tomography (PET)">
  <data key="d0">Positron Emission Tomography (PET)</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Positron emission tomography (PET) is a noninvasive scanning technique that utilizes small amounts of radioactive positrons to visualize body function and metabolism.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fabc67f1dda4a6efe68cb8859b941f8f</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786423078</data>
</node>
<node id="Postpolio Syndrome">
  <data key="d0">Postpolio Syndrome</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Postpolio Syndrome is a severe condition that appears 20–30 years after polio infection, causing slow, progressive muscle weakening in survivors.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c71b04609b9999bd4f4017776dc567f0</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786423103</data>
</node>
<node id="Polio Survivors">
  <data key="d0">Polio Survivors</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Polio Survivors are individuals who previously recovered from polio and may develop Postpolio Syndrome decades later.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c71b04609b9999bd4f4017776dc567f0</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786423103</data>
</node>
<node id="Injured Nerves">
  <data key="d0">Injured Nerves</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Injured Nerves are nerves damaged by polio that may recuperate, regain function, and later fail again in Postpolio Syndrome.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c71b04609b9999bd4f4017776dc567f0</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786423103</data>
</node>
<node id="Paralyzed Muscles">
  <data key="d0">Paralyzed Muscles</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Paralyzed Muscles are muscles that have lost function due to polio-induced nerve damage.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c71b04609b9999bd4f4017776dc567f0</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786423103</data>
</node>
<node id="Stretching Exercise">
  <data key="d0">Stretching Exercise</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Stretching Exercise is a therapeutic activity used to alleviate pain and inflammation in Postpolio Syndrome.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c71b04609b9999bd4f4017776dc567f0</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786423104</data>
</node>
<node id="Pain and Inflammation">
  <data key="d0">Pain and Inflammation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Pain and Inflammation refer to symptoms in muscles and joints that can be treated with medications, hot packs, or stretching exercises in Postpolio Syndrome.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c71b04609b9999bd4f4017776dc567f0</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786423104</data>
</node>
<node id="Asymmetric">
  <data key="d0">Asymmetric</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Asymmetric is a key term describing uneven muscle involvement associated with postpolio syndrome.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c71b04609b9999bd4f4017776dc567f0</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786423105</data>
</node>
<node id="Atrophy">
  <data key="d0">Atrophy</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Atrophy is a key term referring to the wasting away of muscle tissue observed in postpolio syndrome.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c71b04609b9999bd4f4017776dc567f0</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786423105</data>
</node>
<node id="Flaccid">
  <data key="d0">Flaccid</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Flaccid is a key term describing reduced muscle tone that can occur in postpolio syndrome.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c71b04609b9999bd4f4017776dc567f0</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786423105</data>
</node>
<node id="Dr. Prader">
  <data key="d0">Dr. Prader</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b48c45789ebbe48354a0536a248d7e5a</data>
  <data key="d2">Dr. Prader was one of the physicians who first described patients with features of Prader-Willi syndrome.</data>
  <data key="d1">UNKNOWN</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786423142</data>
</node>
<node id="Prader-Willi Syndrome">
  <data key="d0">Prader-Willi Syndrome</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b48c45789ebbe48354a0536a248d7e5a</data>
  <data key="d2">Dr. Prader was one of the physicians who first described patients with features of Prader-Willi syndrome.</data>
  <data key="d1">UNKNOWN</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786423142</data>
</node>
<node id="Weight Gain Control">
  <data key="d0">Weight Gain Control</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b48c45789ebbe48354a0536a248d7e5a</data>
  <data key="d2">Weight gain control is important for the prognosis of Prader-Willi syndrome, influencing life expectancy and health outcomes.</data>
  <data key="d1">UNKNOWN</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786423146</data>
</node>
<node id="Genetic Deletion">
  <data key="d0">Genetic Deletion</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b48c45789ebbe48354a0536a248d7e5a</data>
  <data key="d2">Absence of chromosomal material (genetic deletion) from chromosome 15 causes Prader-Willi syndrome.</data>
  <data key="d1">UNKNOWN</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786423148</data>
</node>
<node id="Precocious Puberty">
  <data key="d0">Precocious Puberty</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Precocious Puberty is a condition characterized by sexual development before age 7 in girls and age 9 in boys, defined by the American Academy of Pediatrics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6409c7c91c51d8723b72f6697dc5c8cc</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786423414</data>
</node>
<node id="Girls">
  <data key="d0">Girls</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Girls are female children who may exhibit early breast development as a sign of Precocious Puberty.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6409c7c91c51d8723b72f6697dc5c8cc</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786423414</data>
</node>
<node id="Boys">
  <data key="d0">Boys</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Boys are male children who may exhibit early testicular enlargement as a sign of Precocious Puberty.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6409c7c91c51d8723b72f6697dc5c8cc</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786423414</data>
</node>
<node id="Gonadotropin‑Releasing Hormone (Gn‑RH)">
  <data key="d0">Gonadotropin‑Releasing Hormone (Gn‑RH)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Gn‑RH is a hormone released by the hypothalamus that triggers the pituitary gland to release gonadotropins.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6409c7c91c51d8723b72f6697dc5c8cc</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786423414</data>
</node>
<node id="Gonadotropins">
  <data key="d0">Gonadotropins</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Gonadotropins are protein hormones that stimulate the gonads to produce sex hormones.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6409c7c91c51d8723b72f6697dc5c8cc</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786423415</data>
</node>
<node id="Testes">
  <data key="d0">Testes</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Testes are male gonads that produce testosterone when stimulated by gonadotropins.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6409c7c91c51d8723b72f6697dc5c8cc</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786423415</data>
</node>
<node id="Premenstrual Syndrome">
  <data key="d0">Premenstrual Syndrome</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-dded7a2364f67a2b68099b4b21e21bdf</data>
  <data key="d2">Estrogen fluctuations are thought to play a role in causing Premenstrual Syndrome symptoms.</data>
  <data key="d1">UNKNOWN</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786423457</data>
</node>
<node id="Premenstrual Dysphoric Disorder">
  <data key="d0">Premenstrual Dysphoric Disorder</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-dded7a2364f67a2b68099b4b21e21bdf</data>
  <data key="d2">Premenstrual Dysphoric Disorder is a severe form of Premenstrual Syndrome.</data>
  <data key="d1">UNKNOWN</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786423459</data>
</node>
<node id="Presbyopia">
  <data key="d0">Presbyopia</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Presbyopia is a vision condition involving loss of the eye’s ability to focus on close objects, occurring as part of normal aging.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9787f8a8b14586557271753b09718b06</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786423491</data>
</node>
<node id="Vision Condition">
  <data key="d0">Vision Condition</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Vision condition refers to a disorder affecting visual function, exemplified by presbyopia.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9787f8a8b14586557271753b09718b06</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786423491</data>
</node>
<node id="Normal Aging">
  <data key="d0">Normal Aging</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Normal aging is the gradual physiological process that occurs over years, during which the eye’s lens loses flexibility.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9787f8a8b14586557271753b09718b06</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786423491</data>
</node>
<node id="Age 40–45">
  <data key="d0">Age 40–45</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Age range 40 to 45 years, when presbyopia symptoms are usually noticeable.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9787f8a8b14586557271753b09718b06</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786423491</data>
</node>
<node id="Age 10–20 Years Later">
  <data key="d0">Age 10–20 Years Later</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Time span of ten to twenty years after symptom onset, when presbyopia stabilizes.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9787f8a8b14586557271753b09718b06</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786423491</data>
</node>
<node id="Loss of Lens Elasticity">
  <data key="d0">Loss of Lens Elasticity</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Loss of elasticity of the eye’s crystalline lens, identified as the major cause of presbyopia.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9787f8a8b14586557271753b09718b06</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786423491</data>
</node>
<node id="Loss of Ciliary Muscle Power">
  <data key="d0">Loss of Ciliary Muscle Power</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Reduced power of the ciliary muscles, considered a contributing cause of presbyopia.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9787f8a8b14586557271753b09718b06</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786423491</data>
</node>
<node id="Ciliary Muscles">
  <data key="d0">Ciliary Muscles</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Ciliary muscles are tiny muscles in the eye that pull and push the lens to adjust its curvature for focusing.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9787f8a8b14586557271753b09718b06</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786423491</data>
</node>
<node id="Crystalline Lens">
  <data key="d0">Crystalline Lens</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">The crystalline lens is a transparent structure behind the iris that focuses light onto the retina.&lt;SEP&gt;The crystalline lens is the transparent lens of the eye that light passes through during Red Reflex Testing.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9787f8a8b14586557271753b09718b06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3db6c2b2025b59f4b58a91f35ae965a1</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786426986</data>
</node>
<node id="Reading Glasses">
  <data key="d0">Reading Glasses</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Reading glasses are eyeglasses designed to aid near vision, used to compensate for presbyopia.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9787f8a8b14586557271753b09718b06</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786423492</data>
</node>
<node id="Bifocal Eyeglasses">
  <data key="d0">Bifocal Eyeglasses</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Bifocal eyeglasses contain two lens powers to correct near and distance vision, used to treat presbyopia.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9787f8a8b14586557271753b09718b06</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786423492</data>
</node>
<node id="Trifocal Eyeglasses">
  <data key="d0">Trifocal Eyeglasses</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Trifocal eyeglasses have three lens zones for near, intermediate, and distance vision, used to treat presbyopia.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9787f8a8b14586557271753b09718b06</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786423492</data>
</node>
<node id="Convex Lens">
  <data key="d0">Convex Lens</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A convex lens is incorporated into glasses to add focusing power for near objects in presbyopia treatment.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9787f8a8b14586557271753b09718b06</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786423492</data>
</node>
<node id="Halfglasses">
  <data key="d0">Halfglasses</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Halfglasses are glasses with the top portion left open for distance vision, used to compensate for presbyopia.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9787f8a8b14586557271753b09718b06</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786423492</data>
</node>
<node id="Contact Lenses">
  <data key="d0">Contact Lenses</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Contact lenses are vision correction devices placed on the eye, prescribed for presbyopia.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9787f8a8b14586557271753b09718b06</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786423492</data>
</node>
<node id="Iris">
  <data key="d0">Iris</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">The iris is the colored part of the eye surrounding the pupil, located in front of the crystalline lens.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9787f8a8b14586557271753b09718b06</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786423492</data>
</node>
<node id="Pupil">
  <data key="d0">Pupil</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">The pupil is the central opening in the iris that allows light to enter the eye.&lt;SEP&gt;The pupil is the opening in the eye through which light enters and appears red during the red reflex test.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9787f8a8b14586557271753b09718b06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3db6c2b2025b59f4b58a91f35ae965a1</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786426985</data>
</node>
<node id="Umbilical Cord">
  <data key="d0">Umbilical Cord</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">The umbilical cord is the structure that connects the embryo or fetus to the placenta, allowing nutrient delivery and waste removal during pregnancy.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7b6708db702ed09e5c157dcbf104366e</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786423508</data>
</node>
<node id="Human Chorionic Gonadotropin (hCG)">
  <data key="d0">Human Chorionic Gonadotropin (hCG)</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7b6708db702ed09e5c157dcbf104366e</data>
  <data key="d2">hCG is a hormone produced during pregnancy and is detected in urine tests to</data>
  <data key="d1">UNKNOWN</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786423509</data>
</node>
<node id="Prepregnancy Counseling">
  <data key="d0">Prepregnancy Counseling</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Prepregnancy counseling is advice supplied by healthcare professionals about steps a mother-to-be and father-to-be can take in preparation for pregnancy, serving as a checklist to ensure a healthy pregnancy.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0562388bfd043886184b76d26796a668</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786423558</data>
</node>
<node id="Obstetrician">
  <data key="d0">Obstetrician</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Obstetrician is a healthcare professional who can provide prepregnancy counseling.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0562388bfd043886184b76d26796a668</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786423558</data>
</node>
<node id="Certified Nurse-Midwife">
  <data key="d0">Certified Nurse-Midwife</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Certified nurse-midwife is a healthcare professional who can provide prepregnancy counseling.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0562388bfd043886184b76d26796a668</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786423558</data>
</node>
<node id="Childbirth Educator">
  <data key="d0">Childbirth Educator</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Childbirth educator is a professional who can provide prepregnancy counseling.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0562388bfd043886184b76d26796a668</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786423558</data>
</node>
<node id="Mother-to-be">
  <data key="d0">Mother-to-be</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Mother-to-be is an individual preparing for pregnancy who receives prepregnancy counseling.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0562388bfd043886184b76d26796a668</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786423558</data>
</node>
<node id="Father-to-be">
  <data key="d0">Father-to-be</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Father-to-be is an individual preparing for pregnancy who receives prepregnancy counseling.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0562388bfd043886184b76d26796a668</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786423558</data>
</node>
<node id="Medication Use">
  <data key="d0">Medication Use</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Medication use is an aspect discussed in prepregnancy counseling concerning safe drug intake before pregnancy.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0562388bfd043886184b76d26796a668</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786423559</data>
</node>
<node id="Lifestyle">
  <data key="d0">Lifestyle</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Lifestyle is an aspect discussed in prepregnancy counseling covering overall habits.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0562388bfd043886184b76d26796a668</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786423559</data>
</node>
<node id="Emotional Health">
  <data key="d0">Emotional Health</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Emotional health is an aspect discussed in prepregnancy counseling concerning mental well‑being.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0562388bfd043886184b76d26796a668</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786423559</data>
</node>
<node id="Birth Control Pills">
  <data key="d0">Birth Control Pills</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Birth control pills are a medication that mother-to-be should stop using several months before conception as part of prepregnancy preparation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0562388bfd043886184b76d26796a668</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786423560</data>
</node>
<node id="Prematurity">
  <data key="d0">Prematurity</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Prematurity is a condition where infants are born before 37 weeks gestation, associated with high risk of complications.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0c63f65bb800d3d7fae8a0864fc3cdf3</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786423565</data>
</node>
<node id="Dubowitz Exam">
  <data key="d0">Dubowitz Exam</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Dubowitz exam is a standardized test scoring responses to 33 neurological stimuli to estimate an infant’s neural development and gestational age.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0c63f65bb800d3d7fae8a0864fc3cdf3</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786423565</data>
</node>
<node id="Intraventricular Hemorrhage">
  <data key="d0">Intraventricular Hemorrhage</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Intraventricular hemorrhage is a condition where blood vessels in the brain burst and bleed into ventricles, associated with prematurity.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0c63f65bb800d3d7fae8a0864fc3cdf3</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786423566</data>
</node>
<node id="Apnea">
  <data key="d0">Apnea</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Apnea is a condition involving a long pause in breathing, associated with prematurity.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0c63f65bb800d3d7fae8a0864fc3cdf3</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786423566</data>
</node>
<node id="Heart Defects">
  <data key="d0">Heart Defects</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Heart defects are cardiovascular abnormalities that can occur in premature infants.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0c63f65bb800d3d7fae8a0864fc3cdf3</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786423566</data>
</node>
<node id="Respiratory Problems">
  <data key="d0">Respiratory Problems</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Respiratory problems are complications affecting the lungs of premature infants.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0c63f65bb800d3d7fae8a0864fc3cdf3</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786423566</data>
</node>
<node id="Blindness">
  <data key="d0">Blindness</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Blindness is a loss of vision that can be a complication of prematurity.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0c63f65bb800d3d7fae8a0864fc3cdf3</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786423566</data>
</node>
<node id="Brain Damage">
  <data key="d0">Brain Damage</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Brain damage refers to injury to the brain that can result from prematurity.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0c63f65bb800d3d7fae8a0864fc3cdf3</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786423566</data>
</node>
<node id="Medical Technology">
  <data key="d0">Medical Technology</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Medical technology includes advances that enable survival of infants born as early as 23 weeks gestational age.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0c63f65bb800d3d7fae8a0864fc3cdf3</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786423566</data>
</node>
<node id="Prenatal Care">
  <data key="d0">Prenatal Care</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Prenatal care involves medical care during pregnancy that can reduce the risk of premature delivery.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0c63f65bb800d3d7fae8a0864fc3cdf3</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786423566</data>
</node>
<node id="Gestational Age">
  <data key="d0">Gestational Age</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Gestational age is the age of the fetus measured from conception, used to determine prematurity.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0c63f65bb800d3d7fae8a0864fc3cdf3</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786423567</data>
</node>
<node id="23 Weeks Gestational Age">
  <data key="d0">23 Weeks Gestational Age</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">23 weeks gestational age is an extremely early stage of pregnancy at which infants may survive with advanced medical technology.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0c63f65bb800d3d7fae8a0864fc3cdf3</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1786423568</data>
