<edge source="source_entity" target="target_entity">
  <data key="d6">194.0</data>
  <data key="d7">(source_entity, target_entity) is an abstract, generic connector used in data‑modeling schemas to indicate that one named entity is linked to another without implying any domain‑specific semantics such as causality, hierarchy, or functional purpose. It functions as a neutral placeholder that allows modelers to record a connection when the precise nature of the relationship is unknown, irrelevant, or deliberately left unspecified, essentially stating “entity A is related to entity B.”  

In most modeling contexts the relation is interpreted as a directed edge, with orientation running from the designated source entity toward the target entity. A minority perspective treats the link as undirected, arguing that the source and target labels may be chosen arbitrarily provided naming rules are followed and duplicate entries are avoided. Both viewpoints are considered valid; consequently, (source_entity, target_entity) can be employed as a directed edge when an explicit orientation is required, or as an undirected link when orientation is irrelevant.  

Naming conventions require each entity name to appear in title case and be capitalized appropriately, while the relation label itself must be written exactly as (source_entity, target_entity). Consistent application of these conventions and the avoidance of duplicate entries are essential to preserve the integrity of the data model.  

All remaining entries in the description list consist solely of placeholder text such as “relationship_description” or “description” and do not provide additional substantive information about the relation.&lt;SEP&gt;relationship_description</data>
  <data key="d8">keywords,relationship_keywords</data>
