<edge source="source" target="target">
  <data key="d6">36.0</data>
  <data key="d7">(source, target) is a generic relational construct used in data models and knowledge graphs to connect a source entity with a target entity. It serves as a connective element between two nodes and imposes strict structural requirements: all mandatory fields must be present, entity names must follow exact capitalization conventions (e.g., “Allergies”), and the relation is case‑sensitive. Each relationship line must adhere to a prescribed syntax, and validation checks ensure that instances conform to the expected schema, naming conventions, and annotation standards.

The relation can be enriched with one or more keywords. Keywords are supplied as a comma‑separated list without extra spaces, allowing concise and correctly punctuated keyword collections. These annotations are part of the validation process, confirming that the keyword‑annotation format complies with the defined standards.

Domain‑specific entities are permitted in the source component; for example, “Aortic Valve Replacement” may appear as a source entity. Users are reminded to include every required entity when constructing the relation, ensuring that no mandatory component is omitted.

The remaining items in the description list consist solely of placeholder text such as “description” or minor fragments that do not add substantive information about the (source, target) relation. Consequently, the core definition, structural rules, case sensitivity, keyword annotation format, validation requirements, and example usage provided in the first description constitute the complete and relevant characterization of the (source, target) relation.</data>
  <data key="d8">keywords,relationship_keywords</data>
